\section{Abstract}

\begin{doublespacing}
\begin{linenumbers}

\noindent\textbf{\textcolor{QUTBlue}{Objectives:}}~This paper presents an exact mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) formulation--a full-horizon decision program that specifies the optimal sequence of Transit Signal Priority (TSP) actions under perfect foresight--cast as a time-expanded timetabling problem: the solution is a schedule of signal-change times that opens each intersection's phases exactly when predicted vehicles need to pass, minimising total predicted passenger delay while keeping every bus on schedule against the published timetable. The MILP is a proof-of-concept formulation and is never solved. \\
\noindent\textbf{\textcolor{QUTBlue}{Methods:}}~The exact MILP is a time-expanded signal timing plan in which the sole decision is the binary phase-state variable $x_{i,p,t}$ (1 if phase $p$ is green at intersection $i$ in second $t$), subject to minimum green, intergreen clearance, cycle-window bounds, queue conservation with finite link storage, a lagged main-green progression overlap, and per-bus timetable-adherence constraints; passenger delay is the single minimised currency ($\rho_{\text{bus}}=40$, $\rho_{\text{car}}=1.2$), lateness is an exact-penalty cap, and progression is an addable linear term. Action letters, offsets, cycle lengths, and green splits are reported outcomes read off the committed schedule, not program variables, which removes the circularity of an action layer inside an omniscient program. The Bellman recursion of the equivalent Markovian process accumulates the predicted delay stage by stage, so the program minimises the total delay under prediction. Every coefficient is a parameter fixed before the solve, keeping the program fully disconnected from the statistical estimation models of the background sections. The approximate methods are a six-rung ladder on one adaptive plan base: the plan alone, a learned bus-weighted policy, an admission-controlled CellQLearn policy, a Nash/GAME bus-priority layer, and a cooperative-game layer, each compared against fixed-time control; every rung keeps the same guards, state feed, and seeds, so the difference between rungs is the priority layer itself. Experiments cover two Brisbane corridors under the same ladder: the 12-intersection Kelvin Grove corridor and the 23-intersection Logan Road corridor, each over five seeds (300--700), each a 1.25-hour peak simulation.\\



\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
% ==============================================================================

Urban traffic congestion imposes significant economic, environmental, and
social costs.  Transit Signal Priority (TSP) improves bus travel time and
schedule adherence at signalised intersections by adjusting signal timing to
favour approaching transit vehicles.  Early implementations relied on
rule-based mechanisms such as early green, green extension, red truncation,
phase insertion, queue jumps, and cycle extension
\citep{Balke2000DevelopmentConcept,Ekeila2009DevelopmentStrategy,Skabardonis2000ControlStrategies,Lin2015ReviewTSP}.

A consistent finding across the classical TSP literature is the
\emph{conditional-priority principle}: priority should be granted only when the
bus is behind schedule or the intervention demonstrably reduces a chosen system
objective, not unconditionally
\citep{Furth2000ConditionalPriority,Ma2010DevelopmentApproach}.  Unconditional
priority has been shown to serve on-time buses that do not need assistance,
worsening general traffic delay without transit benefit.  Even in non-congested
situations, it is common that TSP strategies will at least penalize side-street
traffic \citep{Karthik2023BusStress}.

Recent developments extend beyond rule-based logic to optimization and
learning-based control.  Mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) formulations
provide exact solutions for lane-based bus priority timing \citep{Jin2026BusIntersections}.
Reinforcement learning (RL) approaches offer adaptive policy learning under
stochastic demand conditions \citep{Genders2016UsingControl,Long2022DeepEnvironment,
Hu2023DeepSpeed,Hu2025CoordinatedApproach}.  Game-theoretic formulations model
intersection movements as bargaining agents to achieve equitable delay
distribution \citep{AnsariEsfeh2025AnModel}.  Integrated control strategies
combining lane allocation and signal timing using enhanced cell transmission
models represent a promising direction \citep{Bao2026AnControl}.

Cloud-based TSP systems increasingly rely on General Transit Feed Specification
(GTFS) Realtime data for vehicle location tracking.
\citet{Zhou2026GTFSLatency} demonstrated that GTFS message latency (average
13\,s) and update intervals (average 20\,s) significantly degrade ETA accuracy,
and proposed LSTM-based time-series prediction to compensate for these delays.
Their LSTM-SS model outperforms Kalman filter baselines in single-step distance
increment prediction, motivating its use as an alternative bus position predictor
in the corridor coordinator of the present work.  To address the speed
fluctuation limitations of the standard Kalman filter,
\citet{Ding2022AdaptiveKalman} proposed a time-varying adaptive Kalman filter
(TVAKF) in which the process noise $Q_t$ is scaled by a dynamic factor
computed from an exponentially weighted moving average of recent speed changes.
Their model achieved 2.52\% MAPE for bus travel time prediction, outperforming
both standard Kalman filter and time-series baselines.  We implement TVAKF as a
second alternative predictor alongside LSTM-SS and evaluate all three on the
Kelvin Grove corridor.

\subsection{Literature: bus priority at signalised intersections}
The field consensus is that TSP usually reduces bus delay and travel time and
can improve schedule adherence, but the right treatment depends on what causes
the delay and how constrained the corridor is
\citep{Lin2015ReviewTSP,Skabardonis2000ControlStrategies}.
Conventional timing treatments--early green, green extension, red truncation,
and phase insertion--address signal-allocation delay where some timing
flexibility remains \citep{Ma2008IsolatedEfficiency,Dion2003ArterialTSP}.
Within this group, red truncation is repeatedly reported as more efficient
than green extension at isolated intersections, while phase insertion works
but is highly sensitive to insertion timing and saturation
\citep{Ma2008IsolatedEfficiency}.
Green extension and early green perform best with spare green time and degrade
as the volume-to-capacity ratio rises or coordination quality falls
\citep{Ahn2006GreenExtension,Dion2003ArterialTSP}.
Where buses are trapped in standing queues that timing shifts cannot clear,
queue jumps--dedicated bypass lanes with a pre-signal or advance
area--outperform conventional TSP, with reported bus travel-time reductions
near 30--39\% in modelling studies and larger gains at higher v/c
\citep{Mucsi2008QueueJumps,Lahon2009QueueJumperBRT,Maddu2024QueueJumpVISSIM};
their benefit can exceed the sum of jump plus TSP with near-side stops, but a
bypass just upstream of a downstream bottleneck can harm discharge
\citep{Truong2017MultiplierTSP}.
The clearest development trend is conditional, predictive, AVL/GPS-enabled
priority: serving late buses selectively preserves schedule adherence with far
less disruption than serving every bus
\citep{Furth2000ConditionalPriority,Altun2009SchedulingTSP,Mohammadi2020ConnectedTSP}.
Corridor effects are not the sum of isolated-intersection effects, so selective
deployment and coordination matter: only a subset of intersections may warrant
active priority when the objective is total passenger delay
\citep{Wu2018ArterialTSP,Bayrak2020LocationsTSP}.
Saturation dependence runs through all of this--spare green, stop placement,
dwell time, and downstream bottlenecks decide whether a grant helps or harms
\citep{Wolput2016CycleLength}.
Safety and multimodal effects are real but less integrated into TSP evaluation
than bus and vehicle delay \citep{Goh2013SafetyBenefits,Song2019SafetyTSP,
Cabe2026PedestrianDelay}.

The network-level reading of priority is not new, and the simulation-assignment
literature is where it was worked out.  Bus priority was evaluated inside an
intermodal dynamic network assignment so that the grant and the auto traffic it
diverts are measured in the same model, rather than scored on the treated
approach \citep{Abdelghany2006BusPriority}; the same machinery was extended to
bus rapid transit operations and service planning, where the interaction between
transit service and general traffic has to be represented before a treatment can
be justified \citep{Abdelghany2007BRT}.  For corridors that carry several modes,
the natural unit of management is the corridor itself, with transit and auto
travellers assigned jointly over a multimodal network instead of each junction
being optimised in isolation \citep{Zhou2008MultimodalCorridor}.  This paper
inherits that framing and makes it the evaluation criterion: priority is judged
on corridor passenger delay, with cross-street and downstream costs priced into
the decision rather than reported afterwards.

The present paper adopts exactly this selective, conditional, corridor-aware
position: conditional priority for late buses, person-delay conditions, and
corridor coordination, evaluated with cross-street and downstream costs priced
in.  \cref{tab:lit_actions} places the literature on the review's own
action vocabulary and shows where this paper sits in it.

