Joint optimisation of signal timing and routing has a longer history than the
current per-junction treatments suggest: system-optimal signal timing can be
computed together with time-dependent path assignment, so that the timing is
optimal for the network flow it induces \citep{Abdelfatah1998SystemOptimal}.
What that line of work returns is a plan for a known demand profile, however,
not a per-second decision rule that can be executed against a live bus whose
lateness is only revealed as it arrives; the program of \cref{sec:milp} is the
same network-level ambition stated in a form a controller can be held to.

Despite these advances, most existing studies optimise signal timing
independently of lane allocation, assume static roadway supply, or neglect
spillback-aware corridor-level coordination.  This paper addresses these gaps
through three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item An \textbf{exact MILP formulation} (\cref{sec:milp})
        cast as a time-expanded scheduling problem--intersection settings
        changing per time step--with per-bus timetable-adherence constraints,
        start-variable contiguity, integer queue-waiting delay sums, and
        explicit offset state propagation, and a Markovian (Bellman)
        representation whose transition guards are the rule-based TSP
        mechanisms.  The MILP is a \emph{proof-of-concept formulation}: it
        defines the control problem and is never solved (\cref{sec:milp_status}).
  \item A \textbf{single-program formulation}: every constraint is written in canonical $\le$/$=$ form with no $\min$, $\max$, $\mathrm{abs}$, $\bmod$, or ratio; the action layer, the offset state, and the trigger/Purdue/empty-phase layers are removed from the MILP and relocated to the controller, so the program has one decision ($x_{i,p,t}$), one objective, and one conservation law.
  \item \textbf{Empirical evaluation} on two Brisbane corridors, Kelvin Grove
        (12 intersections) and Logan Road (23 intersections), five seeds each,
        using Aimsun Next microsimulation under the same six-rung
        ladder, guard protocol, and objective.
\end{enumerate}






