\section{Background: Traffic Dynamics Models}
\label{sec:dynamics}

This section presents the underlying physical and statistical models used to
describe delay mechanisms at signalised intersections.  These formulations are
nonlinear but convex in the signal adjustment variable $\delta$.  They are
\emph{background estimation models only}: the exact program of
\cref{sec:milp} is an exact MILP of a simpler point-queue traffic model (see
\cref{sec:milp_boundary}) and consumes these dynamics only through
pre-computed parameters (arrivals, saturation, storage); the online
controllers of \cref{sec:solutions} use them as heuristic state estimators.
Neither the exact program nor its approximations modify these dynamics; the
program never solves them.

% ==========================================================
\subsection{Bus Delay (LWR Kinematic Wave Model)}

Bus delay is modeled using the LWR\footnote{Lighthill, Whitham and Richards}
kinematic wave theory. When a red phase is active, vehicles accumulate
into a standing queue. After the onset of green, the queue dissipates
at the saturation flow rate, generating forward and backward shockwaves.

The queue length at the end of the red phase is given by \cref{eq:Q_end}:
\begin{equation}
  Q_{\text{end}}
  =
  q_{\text{arr}} \, t_{\text{red}}
  \frac{k_{\text{jam}}}{k_{\text{jam}} - k_{\text{arr}}},
  \label{eq:Q_end}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align*}
  q_{\text{arr}} &:= \text{arrival flow rate (veh/s)}, \\
  t_{\text{red}} &:= \text{red phase duration (s)}, \\
  k_{\text{jam}} &:= \text{jam density (veh/km)}, \\
  k_{\text{arr}} &:= \text{arrival traffic density (veh/km)}.
\end{align*}

After the signal turns green, the queue dissipates at the net rate
$(q_{\text{sat}} - q_{\text{arr}})$, yielding a clearance time given by \cref{eq:t_clear}:
\begin{equation}
  t_{\text{clear}}
  =
  \frac{Q_{\text{end}}}{q_{\text{sat}} - q_{\text{arr}}},
  \label{eq:t_clear}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align*}
  q_{\text{sat}} &:= \text{saturation discharge flow rate (veh/s)}.
\end{align*}

Let $\eta_{b,t}$ denote the bus arrival time relative to the start of
the green phase. If the bus arrives before the queue has cleared,
it experiences delay equal to the remaining clearance time.

The resulting bus delay as a function of signal adjustment $\delta$ is
\begin{equation}
  D_{\text{bus}}(\delta)
  =
  \rho_{\text{bus}}
  \max\!\bigl(
    0,\;
    t_{\text{clear}} - \eta_{b,t} + \delta
  \bigr),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align*}
  \delta &:= \text{signal timing adjustment (s)}, \\
  \rho_{\text{bus}} &:= \text{bus delay weighting factor}.
\end{align*}

The function $D_{\text{bus}}(\delta)$ is convex in $\delta$.

% ==========================================================
\subsection{Cross-Traffic Delay (Deterministic D/D/1 Shockwave Model)}

Cross-traffic delay is modeled using a deterministic D/D/1 queue
with shockwave propagation. Extending the opposing phase by $\delta$
seconds increases both queue length and total delay.

Let $Q_0$ denote the initial queue length at the start of the extension.
The total delay experienced by cross-traffic vehicles is

\begin{equation}
  D_{\text{car}}(\delta)
  =
  Q_0 \delta
  +
  \tfrac{1}{2} q_{\text{arr}} \delta^2
  +
  \frac{(Q_0 + q_{\text{arr}}\delta)^2}{2(s - q_{\text{arr}})},
\end{equation}

where
\begin{align*}
  Q_0 &:= \text{initial queue length (veh)}, \\
  q_{\text{arr}} &:= \text{arrival flow rate (veh/s)}, \\
  s &:= \text{saturation discharge rate (veh/s)}, \\
  \delta &:= \text{signal timing adjustment (s)}.
\end{align*}

The first term represents delay to the existing queue,
the second term captures delay from vehicles arriving during
the extension, and the third term accounts for queue
dissipation after the green resumes.

Expanding the above expression yields a quadratic function
\begin{equation}
  D_{\text{car}}(\delta)
  =
  c_1 \delta
  +
  c_2 \delta^2,
\end{equation}
with coefficients
\begin{align*}
  c_1 &= Q_0
        \left(
          1 + \frac{q_{\text{arr}}}{s - q_{\text{arr}}}
        \right), \\
  c_2 &= \frac{q_{\text{arr}}}{2}
        \left(
          1 + \frac{q_{\text{arr}}}{s - q_{\text{arr}}}
        \right).
\end{align*}

Since $s > q_{\text{arr}}$ under stable traffic conditions,
$c_2 > 0$ and the function is convex in $\delta$.

% ==========================================================
\subsection{Moving-Bottleneck Effect}

When buses are stopped within a queue, each queued bus behaves as a moving
bottleneck, increasing headways for following vehicles.
Let $\Bsp_{i,t}$ be the set of buses queued on the main approach of
intersection $i$ at time $t$, with per-intersection queued-bus count
$b_{i,t} := |\Bsp_{i,t}|$, and let $n_{i,t,\beta}^{\text{behind}}$ be the
number of vehicles queued behind bus $\beta \in \Bsp_{i,t}$.
The additional delay imposed on trailing vehicles is modeled as

\begin{equation}
  D_{i,\text{main},\text{car},t}^{\text{mb}}
  =
  \rho_{\text{car}}
  \,
  (h_{\text{bus}} - h_{\text{car}})
  \,
  \sum_{\beta \in \Bsp_{i,t}} n_{i,t,\beta}^{\text{behind}},
\end{equation}

where
\begin{align*}
  \rho_{\text{car}} &:= \text{car delay weighting factor}, \\
  \Bsp_{i,t} &:= \text{set of buses queued on the main approach of $i$ at $t$}, \\
  b_{i,t} := |\Bsp_{i,t}| &:= \text{queued-bus count at intersection $i$ (veh)}, \\
  n_{i,t,\beta}^{\text{behind}} &:= \text{vehicles queued behind bus $\beta$}, \\
  h_{\text{bus}} &:= \text{effective bus headway (s)}, \\
  h_{\text{car}} &:= \text{effective car headway (s)}.
\end{align*}
When at most one bus is queued ($b_{i,t} \le 1$) this reduces to
$\rho_{\text{car}}\,b_{i,t}\,n_{i,t}^{\text{behind}}\,(h_{\text{bus}}-h_{\text{car}})$,
so the old indicator $\mathbf{1}[\text{bus in queue}]$ is simply
$\min(1, b_{i,t})$.

This term represents an additive delay component arising from
capacity reduction caused by queued buses.

% ==========================================================
\subsection{Max-Pressure Control: the Queue-Balancing Baseline}
\label{sec:maxpressure}

Max-pressure control is the canonical responsive traffic signal policy and
serves as the reference point against which any priority mechanism must be
judged \citep{Varaiya2013MaxPressure,Tassiulas1992Stability}.  It requires no
fixed timing plan, no offsets, and no demand knowledge beyond instantaneous
queue lengths: each time it re-evaluates, the controller simply serves the
phase whose activation most reduces total network queue storage.

For intersection $i$, let $n_{i,m}(t)$ denote the queue on the approach lane
group serving movement $m$ (an ordered approach--exit pair) at time $t$, and
let $\mathcal{O}(m)$ be the exit links receiving movement $m$.  The
\emph{pressure} of movement $m$ is its upstream queue minus a weighted share
of the queues it would add downstream:
\begin{equation}
  \pi_{i,m}(t)
  = n_{i,m}(t)
    - \frac{1}{|\mathcal{O}(m)|}\sum_{o \in \mathcal{O}(m)} n_{o}(t),
  \label{eq:mp_movement}
\end{equation}
so a movement is attractive when its own queue is long relative to the space
available to discharge into.  Each phase $p \in \mathcal{P}_i$ releases a set
of movements $\mathcal{M}_p$; the phase pressure is the sum of its movement
pressures,
\begin{equation}
  \Pi_{i,p}(t) = \sum_{m \in \mathcal{M}_p} \max\!\bigl(\pi_{i,m}(t),\,0\bigr),
  \qquad
  p^*_{i}(t) = \arg\max_{p \in \mathcal{P}_i} \Pi_{i,p}(t).
  \label{eq:mp_phase}
\end{equation}
The controller holds $p^*_i(t)$ for a minimum service duration (guarding
against rapid phase changes and preserving pedestrian minima), then re-evaluates.
Two properties explain its enduring appeal: (i) \emph{throughput
optimality}--if demand lies within network capacity, max-pressure keeps all
queues bounded, i.e.\ it stabilises exactly the regime that the $Z_4$
corridor-travel-time objective protects; and (ii) \emph{plan freedom}--it
adapts continuously to demand shifts without retiming.

Its myopia is equally instructive.  Because each decision maximises only the
instantaneous pressure at one intersection, max-pressure is blind to
progression along an arterial: it will happily break a green wave, never
prioritises a late bus (queues contain no passenger or schedule information),
and cannot trade a short-term queue imbalance for a corridor-level benefit.
These are precisely the deficiencies the TSP action space
(\cref{sec:actions}) and the exact program
(\cref{sec:milp}) are designed to overcome--the former by injecting
bus-aware interventions, the latter by optimising across intersections and
over a full planning horizon rather than one decision instant.

Transit-aware and lane-blockage-aware variants of the pressure rule address the
first of these limits by adding passenger weighting, dwelling buses and
permitted left turns to the pressure definition \citep{Kobeissi2026MPTSPLB};
we carry that line into the comparison as the \texttt{MAXPRESSURE\_LB} arm
(\cref{tab:kg_results}), where it narrows but does not close the bus--car
trade-off.

The cell-transmission formulation used to project downstream storage and
the projected-saturation cascade is given in \cref{sec:ctm}.

\section{TSP Action Space}
\label{sec:actions}

The action alphabet is the \emph{candidate generator} of the online
controller, not a decision space of the exact program
(\cref{sec:milp_online_map}).  Each letter is a bounded, integer
perturbation of the launch plan $\bar{x}_{i,p,t}$; the program
of \cref{sec:milp} decides the signal plan itself, and the controller
chooses a letter that approximates it one event at a time.  Our codes map to
the classical review vocabulary \citep{Lin2015ReviewTSP,Ma2008IsolatedEfficiency} as:
\texttt{GE} = green extension, \texttt{ER} = early green / early red,
\texttt{GR} = red truncation by reallocation, \texttt{INS/PI} = phase
insertion, offset and progression coupling = offset correction/optimization,
green/cycle bounds plus \texttt{GR} re-splits = split/cycle adjustment, and
none of our scored arms provides a queue-jump bypass lane
\citep{Mucsi2008QueueJumps,Maddu2024QueueJumpVISSIM}--queue-trapped delay is
handled here by timing only.  Full coverage against the review is tabulated
in \cref{tab:lit_actions}.

\paragraph{Terminology.}
A \emph{phase} is a set of movements that receive right of way together; it is
\emph{green} when it has right of way and \emph{red} otherwise. A
\emph{signal timing plan} assigns a phase state to every intersection and
second; the binary variable $x_{i,p,t}=1$ means that phase $p$ is green at
intersection $i$ in second $t$. We do not use ``stage'' (the UK synonym for a
phase) or ``gate'' (which is not a signal state), and we reserve ``gating''
for the admission rules that decide whether a proposed action is allowed.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Action & Type & Description \\
\hline
\texttt{NO\_ACTION} & Passive & No intervention \\
\texttt{GE}  & Green Extension & Prolong bus-phase green +$\delta$\,s \\
\texttt{GR}  & Green Reallocation & Shorten non-bus phase to advance bus green \\
\texttt{INS} & Phase Insertion & Insert $\delta$\,s bus phase into cycle \\
\texttt{ER}  & Early Red & Terminate bus phase early (bus already cleared) \\
\texttt{VP}  & Variable Phase (Skip) & Select next phase; skip intermediate phases \\
\texttt{PT}  & Phase Rotation & Select optimal phase sequence from candidate set \\
\texttt{OC}  & Offset Correction & Shift the coordinated offset by $\Delta$ so the bus arrives on green \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

