Every timing magnitude is an integer at $1\,$s resolution. signals commit
whole seconds (the controller writes integer durations), so the tokens are
$\texttt{GE}_{\delta}$ with $\delta \in \Z_{\ge 1}$ in the admissible band.
$\delta_{i,k} \in [\delta_k^{\min}, \delta_k^{\max}]$ is a contiguous integer
range, not a grid.  Variable-phase (VP) and phase-rotation
(PHASE\_ROT) actions extend the action space with phase-sequence control,
described in Sections~\ref{sec:phase_skip} and~\ref{sec:phase_rot}.

\noindent\textbf{GE acts on the live green.} Green extension lengthens whichever
phase is green \emph{now}, so on a red bus phase it lengthens the conflicting
green, pushes the bus green further into the future, and leaves the bus worse
off than under no action.  GE is therefore gated on phase validity
(\cref{eq:online_phase}), one of the runtime guards of
\cref{sec:online_guards}: GE only while the bus phase is live, insertion or no
action otherwise.

\subsection{Variable Phase Action and Phase Skipping}
\label{sec:phase_skip}

The standard action set \{\texttt{NO\_ACTION, GE, GR, INS, ER}\} does not
allow the controller to alter the \emph{sequence} of phases within a cycle.
Variable-phase and phase-rotation actions extend the alphabet with
phase-sequence control; they are online-only extensions of the signal plan
that \cref{sec:milp} defines, and they are subject to the same capacity
legality (\cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds}) and the spillback/queue guards of
\cref{sec:online_guards}.


The standard action set \{\texttt{NO\_ACTION, GE, GR, INS, ER}\} does not
allow the controller to alter the \emph{sequence} of phases within a cycle.
Three related terms appear below and mean different things.
The Variable Phase (VP) \emph{mechanism} of Long et al.
\citep{Long2022PhaseSkipping} adds a phase-selection action: at each decision
step, the controller chooses the \emph{next} phase index from $\Lambda_i =
\{0, 1, \dots, |\mathcal{P}_i|-1\}$.
\emph{Phase skipping} is the resulting \emph{effect}: a phase is served zero
green because it was not selected next, subject to the service guards below.
\emph{Phase rotation} (Sec.~\ref{sec:phase_rot}) is the cycle-level version:
reordering the whole sequence once per cycle rather than choosing the next
phase step by step.

\paragraph{Formal definition.}
At intersection $i$ and step $t$, the VP action selects:
\begin{equation}
  a_{i,t}^{\mathrm{VP}} \in \Lambda_i
    = \{0, 1, \dots, |\mathcal{P}_i|-1\}
  \label{eq:vp_action}
\end{equation}
The controller skips phase $p$ if $p$ is not selected as the next served phase,
advancing directly to the chosen phase after the current intergreen.

\paragraph{Phase-skipping guards (invalid-action masking).}
Not all elements of $\Lambda_i$ are admissible at every step. Two service
guards from \citep{Long2022PhaseSkipping} restrict the selectable set. They are
bus-priority policy, not safety constraints: genuine safety (minimum greens,
intergreen clearance, pedestrian times) is enforced separately by the
capacity-legality bounds R7. What follows decides when skipping is
\emph{worthwhile}, never when it is \emph{safe}.

Let $L_{Q,p}(t)=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}_p} q_{i,m}(t)$ be the vehicles queued on
the movements that phase $p$ serves, $n_{i,p}(t)$ the vehicles detected on
$p$'s approach in the current detection window, and $a^{\mathrm{VP}}_{i,t-1}$
the phase selected at the previous step. Define the skip-admissible indicator
$\sigma_{i,p}(t)\in\{0,1\}$, with $\sigma_{i,p}(t)=1$ meaning that phase $p$
may be skipped at step $t$:
\begin{align}
  \sigma^{(q)}_{i,p}(t) &= \mathbf{1}\!\left[\,L_{Q,p}(t)\le Q^{\mathrm{skip}}_{\max}\,\right],
    \label{eq:skip_queue}\\
  \sigma^{(c)}_{i,p}(t) &= \mathbf{1}\!\left[\,a^{\mathrm{VP}}_{i,t-1}=p
    \;\lor\; n_{i,p}(t)=0\,\right],
    \label{eq:skip_consec}\\
  \sigma_{i,p}(t) &= \sigma^{(q)}_{i,p}(t)\,\sigma^{(c)}_{i,p}(t).
    \label{eq:skip_admissible}
\end{align}
Equation~\eqref{eq:skip_queue} forbids skipping a phase whose queue exceeds
$Q^{\mathrm{skip}}_{\max}$, which prevents starvation of a loaded approach; the
indicator is one when the phase is light enough to be skipped.
Equation~\eqref{eq:skip_consec} forbids skipping a phase in two consecutive
steps while demand remains on it.

Choosing $p$ as the next served phase skips every other phase, so $p$ is
admissible as the next phase only if every $p'\ne p$ is skip-admissible. Let
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{M}_i(t) = \{\,p\in\Lambda_i : \sigma_{i,p}(t)=0\,\}
  \label{eq:must_serve}
\end{equation}
be the phases that must be served next. The invalid-action mask is
\begin{equation}
  \Lambda_i^*(t) =
  \begin{cases}
    \mathcal{M}_i(t), & \mathcal{M}_i(t)\ne\emptyset,\\[2pt]
    \Lambda_i, & \mathcal{M}_i(t)=\emptyset,
  \end{cases}
  \label{eq:iam}
\end{equation}
and the controller selects $a^{\mathrm{VP}}_{i,t}\in\Lambda_i^*(t)$ only, so
masked actions are never evaluated or executed. This invalid action masking
(IAM) \citep{Long2022PhaseSkipping} is essential for starvation-free
phase-sequence control: without it, a learning controller could repeatedly
skip a high-demand phase, causing queue spillback.

\subsection{Phase Rotation}
\label{sec:phase_rot}

Phase rotation \citep{Yu2022PhaseRotation} jointly optimises the phase
\emph{sequence}, cycle length, and green splits, rather than accepting the
baseline phase order as fixed.  A set $\mathcal{R}_i$ of candidate sequences
is enumerated offline; at each cycle $T$, the controller selects one:

\paragraph{Binary sequence selection.}
At intersection $i$ and cycle $T$, let $z_{i,r}^T \in \{0,1\}$ indicate
whether candidate sequence $r \in \mathcal{R}_i$ is implemented:
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{r \in \mathcal{R}_i} z_{i,r}^T = 1
  \qquad \forall\,i \in \mathcal{I},\; T
  \label{eq:rot_select}
\end{equation}
Exactly one sequence is active per cycle.  For a standard four-phase
intersection, $|\mathcal{R}_i| = 3$ distinct cyclic orderings are typically
sufficient; a six-phase intersection admits up to $|\mathcal{R}_i| = 8$
candidate sequences \citep{Yu2022PhaseRotation}.

\paragraph{Joint green-split optimisation.}
For each candidate sequence $r \in \mathcal{R}_i$ the green times
$g_{i,p,T}$ are generated by a Webster-type allocation
\citep{Webster1958Traffic}.  This is a \emph{candidate-generation rule}, not a
second objective of the exact program: it fixes the split at which a candidate
sequence is evaluated, so that sequences are compared on a like-for-like
basis, and the decision itself remains the binary choice of
\cref{eq:rot_select}.  The allocation, its closed form, and the approximation
used when the minimum greens bind are given in \cref{app:webster}.  The
sequence with the lowest predicted cycle delay is then selected (exactly one
active sequence per cycle, \cref{eq:rot_select}).

