\paragraph{Decision variable.} The launch plan fixes only the initial
condition---\emph{when} the plan says each phase should be green---together with
the cycle, green, and storage bounds of the timing family. The sole decision variable of
the program is the time-indexed green/red indicator
$x_{i,p,t} \in \{0,1\}$.  Nothing in the objective is
chosen---only the phase-state trajectory is---so every reported quantity is a
consequence of that trajectory rather than an input to it.  Action letters,
offsets, cycle lengths, and green splits are \emph{derived} quantities:
they are read off the committed signal plan and are used by the online
controller of \cref{sec:solutions}; they are \emph{not} variables of this
program.  This is deliberate: in a full-horizon program the signal plan is the
decision, and an action vocabulary is a language for proposing schedules, not
a decision space.

\paragraph{Integer time.} Time is integer-indexed throughout this section.
The step is fixed to $\Delta t = 1$\,s and the horizon is
$\Tsp = \{0,1,\dots,T\} \subset \Z_{\ge 0}$; every duration, green, travel
time, and arrival clock is an integer number of steps (seconds).  No
continuous time variable appears in the exact program.

\paragraph{Scope of the formulation.}
The program addresses a single question: given perfect foreknowledge of every
arrival, queue, and timetable over the horizon, which phase should be green at
every intersection and second so that total passenger delay is minimised while
each bus meets its timetable? Its inputs are the second-by-second street state:
the active signal aspect, queue lengths and discharge rates, each intersection's
position in its cycle relative to its neighbours, each bus's schedule deviation,
and the resulting passenger delay. Its outputs are the phase state for every
phase and second, together with the passage time of each bus. The program does
not estimate, learn, or search; detectors, triggers, cooldowns, spillback
checks, congestion vetoes, Q-values, Harmony Search, Nash utilities, measured
feeds, and the $Z_1$-selection rule are not constraints of the program. They
belong to the online solution methods described in \cref{sec:solutions}.

\subsection{Time-Expanded Interpretation}
\label{sec:sched_interp}

\paragraph{Definition of the traffic model.}
Throughout this section the \emph{traffic model} is the deterministic
integer-time model of the intersection: the signal phase and clearance
constraints, the green and cycle bounds, the queue, discharge, waiting, and
storage recursions, and network conservation. These are the \emph{model
constraints}: they state what the road and the signal do when a phase is green,
and they contain no transit logic.
The TSP letters \texttt{GE}, \texttt{INS}, \texttt{ER}, \texttt{GR},
\texttt{VP}, \texttt{PT}, \texttt{OC} are a \emph{policy vocabulary}: they
select or perturb the signal plan $x$. They are not constraints of the
traffic model and do not appear in the program. Likewise the launch plan
$\bar{x}$ is a feasible initial signal plan (the launch plan), not a
constraint, and the online guards R1--R11 of \cref{sec:rule_tsp} belong to
the policy layer, not to the traffic model. ``Delay-only'' below therefore means the
program restricted to the constraints just listed, with the delay criterion
$Z_1$.

