Signal-caused queueing delay, occupancy-weighted & \textbf{Minimised} (the one objective) \\
$Z_2$ green-wave progression & \cref{eq:Z2} &
Lagged main-green overlap & Optional linear term at fixed coefficient \\
$Z_3$ aggregate lateness (steps$\times$bus) & \cref{eq:Z3} &
Timetable violation of every bus & Exact-penalty cap \\
$Z_4$ corridor travel time (veh\,h) & \cref{eq:Z4} &
End-to-end bus and car travel time & Reported metric, not constrained here \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Minimised: total person delay ($Z_1$).}
Person delay is the single currency throughout: occupancy times vehicle
delay.  With $\rho_v$ the occupancy (pax/veh) of class $v$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:person_delay}
  P_{i,t} = \sum_{s \in \Ssp} \sum_{v \in \Vsp} \rho_v\, D_{i,s,v,t}
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp,
\end{equation}
in person-seconds.  $Z_1$ is person delay with an extra section weight $w_s$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z1}
  Z_1 = \sum_{t \in \Tsp} \sum_{i \in \Isp}
    \Bigl( w_{\text{main}}\,\rho_{\text{bus}}\, D_{i,\text{main},\text{bus},t}
    + w_{\text{main}}\,\rho_{\text{car}}\, D_{i,\text{main},\text{car},t}
    + w_{\text{side}}\,\rho_{\text{car}}\, D_{i,\text{side},\text{car},t} \Bigr).
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Progression term ($Z_2$).}
With lagged overlap $h_{ij,t}$ and main-platoon flow $q_{ij,t}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z2}
  Z_2 = \sum_{t \in \Tsp} \sum_{(i,j) \in \Esp}
    q_{ij,t}\, h_{ij,t}.
\end{equation}
Perfect overlap scores $q$ per step; the progression tolerance is enforced
by the minimum overlap $B^{\min}_{ij}$ of \cref{eq:band_min}.  By default
$Z_2$ is monitored and not optimised; if the modeller chooses to price
progression it enters the one objective as $-\omega_2 Z_2$ at a fixed
coefficient $\omega_2$---overlap is rewarded, hence the minus sign
(\cref{eq:opt_crit,tab:objective_terms}).

\paragraph{Capped: schedule adherence ($Z_3$).}
Lateness is $\sigma_{b,i}^+ = \max(0, \tau_{b,i} - \bar{\tau}_{b,i})$
(\cref{eq:sched_split}); earliness is free:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z3}
  Z_3 = \sum_{b \in \Bsp} \sum_{i \in \Rcal_b} \omega_{b}\, \sigma_{b,i}^+,
  \qquad \omega_b \ge 1.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Reported: corridor travel time ($Z_4$).}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z4}
  Z_4 = TT^{\text{bus}} + TT^{\text{car}}, \qquad
  TT^{v} = \sum_{n \in \mathcal{N}^v} \bigl(t_n^{\text{exit}} - t_n^{\text{entry}}\bigr),
  \quad v \in \{\text{bus},\text{car}\}.
\end{equation}
$Z_4$ is a post-run veh\,h outcome (network bus and car total-time
statistics), never a per-step variable and not a constraint of this program.
The throughput cap is enforced in the online selection layer, not here.

\subsubsection{Optimality Criterion}
\label{sec:objective_crit}
The program carries one minimised scalar. $\omega_2 \ge 0$ is a fixed
coefficient set before the solve---$\omega_2 = 0$ is the delay-only default
and any $\omega_2 > 0$ simply adds the $Z_2$ term of
\cref{tab:objective_terms}---so it is data, not a mode and not a tuned online
weight:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:opt_crit}
  \min_{x,Q,d,Q^w,D,e,\tau,\sigma,h,s^3}\;
  Z_1 + \lambda_3 \sum_{b \in \Bsp}\sum_{i \in \Rcal_b} s^3_{b,i}
  - \omega_2 Z_2
  \quad \text{s.t.} \quad
  \text{constraints \cref{eq:gate_excl,eq:sched_cap}},\;
  \sigma^+_{b,i} \le \epsilon^{\text{late}} + s^3_{b,i}\;\; \forall b,i.
\end{equation}
The lateness cap is enforced through the slack $s^3$ priced at
$\lambda_3$: when $\lambda_3$ exceeds the largest possible delay saving
attributable to one late second, a unit of lateness is never traded for less
than a unit of delay, so the cap is exact whenever it is feasible and the
program falls back to minimising lateness only when the cap is infeasible.
The all--no-action schedule always satisfies physical feasibility and the
slacks, so the program degrades gracefully to fixed time under saturation.

\subsection{Constraints}
\label{sec:milp_constraints}

\paragraph{Index conventions.} Throughout \cref{sec:milp}: $i,j \in \Isp$
intersections; $t \in \Tsp$ integer steps; $p \in \Psp_i$ phases
($p_b^i$ bus phase); $m \in \Msp_i$ movements; $v \in \Vsp$ classes;
$b \in \Bsp$ buses with route $\Rcal_b$; $s \in \Ssp$ sections;
$(i,j) \in \Esp$ links; $c$ cycle windows.  Every constraint below is
written in canonical form $a^\top z \le b$ or $a^\top z = b$: lower bounds
appear with negated coefficients.  No $\min$, $\max$, $\mathrm{abs}$,
$\bmod$, or ratio appears in any constraint; products of a binary and a
bounded variable are avoided by construction.

\paragraph{Signal-switching physics.}
\begin{align}
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} x_{i,p,t} &\le 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_excl} \\[3pt]
  G_i^{\min}\,(x_{i,p,t} - x_{i,p,t-1}) - \sum_{r=t}^{\min(t+G_i^{\min}-1,T)} x_{i,p,r} &\le 0
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_dwell} \\[3pt]
  x_{i,p,t} + x_{i,p',t+r} &\le 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; (p,p') \in \mathcal{F}_i,\; r=1,\dots,I_{\text{ig}},\; t \le T-r \label{eq:gate_clear}
\end{align}

\paragraph{Green and cycle bounds.}
\begin{align}
  -\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le -G_i^{\min}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; c \label{eq:g_bounds} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le G_i^{\max}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; c \\[3pt]
  -\sum_{p \in \Psp_i}\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le -(C_i^{\min}-L_i)
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; c \label{eq:c_bounds} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i}\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le C_i^{\max}-L_i
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; c
\end{align}

\paragraph{Flow and storage.}
\begin{align}
  Q_{i,m,v,t+1} - Q_{i,m,v,t} - A_{i,m,v,t} + d_{i,m,v,t} &= 0
    && \forall i,m,v,\; t < T \label{eq:mv_q} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{v \in \Vsp} d_{i,m,v,t} - \mu_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t} &\le 0
    && \forall i,m,t \label{eq:mv_dcap} \\[3pt]
  d_{i,m,v,t} - Q_{i,m,v,t} - A_{i,m,v,t} &\le 0
    && \forall i,m,v,t \\[3pt]
  Q^w_{i,m,v,t} - Q_{i,m,v,t} &\le 0 \label{eq:mv_wait} \\[3pt]
  Q^w_{i,m,v,t} + K_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t} &\le K_{i,m} \\[3pt]
  Q_{i,m,v,t} - Q^w_{i,m,v,t} - K_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t} &\le 0 \\[3pt]
  \sum_{m \in \Msp_i(s,v)} Q^w_{i,m,v,t} - D_{i,s,v,t} &\le 0 \label{eq:mv_waitsum} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{v \in \Vsp} Q_{i,m,v,t} - K_{i,m} &\le 0 \label{eq:storage_cap} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{v \in \Vsp} d_{i,m,v,t} + \sum_{v \in \Vsp} Q_{j,m^+(m),v,t} - K_{j,m^+(m)} + M^{\text{big}}\,(1 - x_{i,p(m),t}) &\le M^{\text{big}}
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; m,\; t \label{eq:discharge_space}
\end{align}
where $A_{i,m,v,t}$ is the realised arrival count (endogenous through
\cref{eq:cons_phase}) and $p(m)$ is the phase serving movement $m$.

\paragraph{Network conservation.}
\begin{align}
  N^{\text{in}}_{ij,v,t} - n_{ij,v,t} - \sum_{m \in \Msp_i:\, m \to (i,j)} d_{i,m,v,t-\tau_{ij}} &= 0
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; v,\; t \label{eq:cons_link} \\[3pt]
  A_{j,m,v,t} - \sum_{i:\,(i,j)\in\Esp} \gamma^m_{ij}\, N^{\text{in}}_{ij,v,t} - n^m_{j,v,t} &= 0
    && \forall j,m,v,t \label{eq:cons_phase}
\end{align}
where $\gamma^m_{ij} \in \{0,1\}$ is the fixed geometry mapping link $(i,j)$
to movement $m$ at $j$, and terms with $t-\tau_{ij}<0$ are zero.

\paragraph{Corridor coordination (lagged progression).}
\begin{align}
  h_{ij,t} - x_{i,p_b^i,t} &\le 0 \label{eq:band_min} \\[3pt]
  h_{ij,t} - x_{j,p_b^j,t+\ell_{ij}} &\le 0 \\[3pt]
  x_{i,p_b^i,t} + x_{j,p_b^j,t+\ell_{ij}} - 1 - h_{ij,t} &\le 0 \\[3pt]
  -\sum_{t \in \Tsp} h_{ij,t} &\le -B^{\min}_{ij}
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp
\end{align}
with $x_{j,p,t+\ell} := 0$ when $t+\ell > T$.

\paragraph{Bus clocks and schedule adherence.}
\begin{align}
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} e_{b,i,t} - 1 &= 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:arr_assign} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} t\, e_{b,i,t} - \tau_{b,i} &= 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:arr_link} \\[3pt]
  e_{b,i,t} - x_{i,p_b^i,t} &\le 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:prog_bus} \\[3pt]
  \tau_{b,i_h} + \tau^{\text{ff}}_{i_h,i_{h+1}} - \tau_{b,i_{h+1}} &\le 0
    && \forall b,\; h=0,\dots,N-1 \label{eq:arr_prop} \\[3pt]
  \tau_b^{\text{detect}} - \tau_{b,i_0} &\le 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp \label{eq:arr_init} \\[3pt]
  \tau_{b,i} - \bar{\tau}_{b,i} - \sigma^+_{b,i} + \sigma^-_{b,i} &= 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_split} \\[3pt]
  \sigma^+_{b,i} - s^3_{b,i} &\le \epsilon^{\text{late}}
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_cap}
\end{align}

\paragraph{Domains.}
\begin{align*}
  & x_{i,p,t} \in \{0,1\},\quad h_{ij,t} \in \{0,1\},\quad e_{b,i,t} \in \{0,1\}, \\
  & Q_{i,m,v,t}, d_{i,m,v,t}, Q^w_{i,m,v,t}, D_{i,s,v,t}, s^3_{b,i} \in \Z_{\ge 0}, \\
  & \tau_{b,i}, \sigma^\pm_{b,i} \in \Z_{\ge 0}.
\end{align*}

\paragraph{No re-optimisation is executed.}
\label{sec:offset_recourse}
Every evaluated method solves the program once, over the fixed horizon, and
holds the resulting plan for the whole run. The receding-horizon counterpart
would solve the same program over a window
$\Tsp(t_c)=\{t_c,\dots,t_c+T_w\}$ with the fixed initial state replaced by the
measured state $\hat{X}_{t_c}$:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{P}\bigl(\hat{X}_{t_c},\,\Tsp(t_c)\bigr)
  \;=\;
  \mathcal{P}\big|_{\,\Tsp(t_c),\; X_{t_c}=\hat{X}_{t_c}} .
  \label{eq:reopt}
\end{equation}
Equation~\eqref{eq:reopt} defines the online decision problem; it is not a
description of what any evaluated method runs. None of the six methods runs an
MILP inside the simulation loop (Sec.~\ref{sec:milp_status}), so none of them has
a re-optimisation schedule, an incumbent, or a warm start to report. The plan
layer of Sec.~\ref{sec:layered_control} is not a re-solve either: it selects, at
each epoch, one plan from a fixed library of eight candidates by a scored
argmax, and a selection carries no solver state from one epoch to the next, so
there is nothing to warm-start.

\emph{Remark (about \eqref{eq:reopt}, not about the experiments).}
Were \eqref{eq:reopt} executed, the only object carried between epochs would be
an initial incumbent: with $X_{t_c}=\hat{X}_{t_c}$ and the all-no-action
continuation feasible, a shifted previous schedule is a valid starting point,
though feasibility of each window follows from the continuation constraint, not
from that starting point. Offsets are then read off each plan as in the reported
quantities below; the difference between them is the shift an offset-correction
action \texttt{OC} would apply, and the controller of Sec.~\ref{sec:solutions}
decides whether to take it without solving the program.

\paragraph{Reported (non-constraint) quantities.} Realised green is the open
indicator itself,
\begin{equation}
  g_{i,p,t} = x_{i,p,t} \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:gate_green}
\end{equation}
and the following are read off the committed schedule and are not variables of
the program:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Offset} $\phi_{i,c}$: the first step $t \in W_{i,c}$ with
        $x_{i,p_b^i,t}=1$.
  \item \textbf{Realised green} $g_{i,p,t} = x_{i,p,t}$, so $g=x$ (\cref{eq:gate_green}).
  \item \textbf{Green split} $G_{i,p,c} = \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t}$.
  \item \textbf{Cycle} $C_{i,c} = \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} G_{i,p,c} + L_i$.
  \item \textbf{Action label} at $(i,t)$: the letter ($\texttt{GE}$,
        $\texttt{INS}$, $\texttt{ER}$, $\texttt{GR}$, $\texttt{VP}$,
        $\texttt{PT}$, $\texttt{OC}$, $\texttt{NA}$) whose phase perturbation
        best matches the change in $x_{i,\cdot,t}$ relative to the launch plan
        $\bar{x}_{i,\cdot,t}$; the controller of
        \cref{sec:solutions} chooses the letter directly.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Equivalent Markovian Formulation}
\label{sec:markovian}

This subsection recasts the program defined in this section as a finite-horizon dynamic program over an estimated state.

\paragraph{State: the model's memory.}
A state is sufficient only if it carries every quantity that a later
constraint can read. The reduced tuple $(x_{t-1},Q_t,\tau_t)$ used in an
earlier draft is \emph{not} sufficient: minimum green and intergreen are
window constraints, the green/cycle bounds are per-cycle counts, the
progression overlap reads a progression lag, and buses upstream of a junction are
not in $\tau_t$. The sufficient state is
\begin{equation}
  X_t=\bigl(x_{t-1},\ \kappa_t,\ \zeta_t,\ W_t,\ Q_t,\ N_t,\ T_t\bigr),
  \label{eq:markov_state}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item $x_{t-1}=\{x_{i,p,t-1}\}$ is the previous phase-state vector;
  \item $\kappa_t$ holds the remaining minimum-green and intergreen counters
        per $(i,p)$ and $(i,p,p')$ (non-negative integers bounded by
        $G_i^{\min}$ and $I_{\text{ig}}$);
  \item $\zeta_t$ holds the current cycle-window index and the green-step
        counts per $(i,p,c)$ required by \cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds};
  \item $W_t=(x_{t-1},\dots,x_{t-\ell_{\max}})$ is the history of phase states over the
        longest progression lag
        $\ell_{\max}=\max_{(i,j)\in\Esp}\ell_{ij}$;
  \item $Q_t=\{Q_{i,m,v,t}\}$ collects the movement queues, including the
        downstream queues read by the storage and discharge constraints;
  \item $N_t=\{N^{\text{in}}_{ij,v,t}\}$ is the in-transit pipeline on every
        link, i.e.\ the vehicles already discharged upstream but not yet
        arrived downstream; it carries the free-flow lag $\tau_{ij}$ of the
        network conservation \cref{eq:cons_link};
  \item $T_t$ collects the clocks and positions of \emph{all} buses that
        have entered the network, not only those past a junction.
\end{itemize}
Every entry is integer or a finite binary vector, so the state is finite.

\paragraph{Transition and stage cost.}
The control is
$u_t=\{x_{i,p,t}\}_{i,p}\in\{0,1\}^{\sum_{i\in\Isp}|\Psp_i|}$. To make the flow a
function of the state and control, impose the model's \emph{service closure}:
\begin{equation}
  (S)\qquad
  d_{i,m,v,t}=\text{the maximal feasible discharge at saturation }
  \mu_{i,m}x_{i,p(m),t}
  \text{ in a fixed class order,}
  \label{eq:service_closure}
\end{equation}
subject to available vehicles and downstream storage. Under $(S)$ the
discharge is not a free decision; it is the rate at which the model serves a
green movement. Then
\begin{equation}
  X_{t+1}=F(X_t,u_t)
  \label{eq:markov_trans}
\end{equation}
is deterministic: $Q$ follows \cref{eq:mv_q}, $d$ follows $(S)$, $Q^w$
follows \cref{eq:mv_wait} ($Q^w=Q$ on red, $0$ on green), the counters
follow their definitions, and the bus clocks follow \cref{eq:arr_prop} with
the \emph{first-green passage rule} (a bus crosses at the first step at
which it is at the stop line and its phase is green).

The stage cost is the delay of step $t$:
\begin{equation}
  c_t(X,u)=\sum_{i\in\Isp}\sum_{s\in\Ssp}\sum_{v\in\Vsp}
    w_s\,\rho_v\sum_{m\in\Msp_i(s,v)} Q^w_{i,m,v,t}(X,u),
  \label{eq:stage_cost}
\end{equation}
which is $Z_1$ of \cref{eq:Z1} term by term once $D$ is at its lower bound
\cref{eq:mv_waitsum}; the bound is tight at any optimum because
$w_s,\rho_v>0$.

\paragraph{The full criterion.}
The scored program also carries the progression term $-\omega_2 Z_2$ and the
lateness penalty $\lambda_3\sum s^3$ of \cref{eq:opt_crit}. Both are
stage-additive under the same state: $h_{ij,t-\ell_{ij}}$ is read from $W_t$
and $u_t$, the overlap count is one of the counters in $\zeta_t$ (with the
terminal requirement $\sum_t h_{ij,t}\ge B^{\min}_{ij}$), and
$\sigma^+_{b,i}$ is read from $T_t$ when bus $b$ passes $i$. Adding those
terms to $c_t$ and imposing the terminal count constraint leaves the program
time-expanded and unchanged in structure. It is not evaluated as a dynamic
program: the controller applies the measured state and the guards of
\cref{sec:online_guards}, and the program is reported as a time-expanded MILP
and never solved as a DP.

\paragraph{Determinism of the auxiliary variables.}
Under the service closure $(S)$ and a fixed phase sequence, the state
trajectory $X_t$ is uniquely determined by $X_0$, and the auxiliaries
$(D,h,e,\tau,\sigma^\pm,s^3)$ are determined at an optimum:
$Q_{t+1}$ follows \cref{eq:mv_q}; $d$ follows $(S)$; $Q^w$ follows
\cref{eq:mv_wait}; $D$ follows \cref{eq:mv_waitsum}, and at an optimum the
bound is tight because $w_s,\rho_v>0$; $h$ follows from the history of phase
states; $e,\tau$ follow the first-green passage rule; and $\sigma^\pm,s^3$
follow \cref{eq:sched_split,eq:sched_cap}. Two qualifications matter. First,
in the raw inequality system $d$ is a free integer variable; $(S)$ is what
turns it into a function, so the statement fails without $(S)$. Second, $D$
is tight only at an optimum; away from the optimum \cref{eq:mv_waitsum} can
be slack, which does not affect the optimum because the objective is
increasing in $D$. With those two points $F$ is well defined and no extra
history variable is needed beyond \cref{eq:markov_state}.

\subsection{Arrival Uncertainty}
\label{sec:stochastic}

\paragraph{What is observed, and what is not.}
The corridor is instrumented, but the instruments are not equally informative, so
the program treats three kinds of quantity differently. The \emph{bus trajectory}
is monitored: each equipped bus is tracked every step, so $\tau_{b,i}$ is a
measurement at the $1$\,s resolution of the program's own step, not a forecast.
The \emph{car arrival count} is not observed---only counted over a detector
window---so that is where the uncertainty belongs. The \emph{car queue} is
partially observed: a detector reports whether an approach holds a vehicle at
all, and a null observation is information the program can carry as a constraint
rather than as a small number. Uncertainty is therefore attached to cars. A bus
is where the sensor says it is. This is deliberately not a two-stage,
chance-constrained, or sample-average program---no scenario variable, no recourse
variable, and no probability enters the constraint matrix
(\cref{sec:milp_status}).

\paragraph{Buses: measured, not predicted.}
For every bus already in the network, $\tau_{b,i}$ is the measured passage step
and enters the program as data. The one bus time that is genuinely unknown is the
entry of a bus that has not yet reached the corridor, and because that bus is
tracked from the upstream stop line the unknown window is a single sampling
interval,
\begin{equation}
  \tau_{b,i_0} \;=\; \hat{\tau}_{b,i_0} \pm \varsigma_b,
  \qquad \varsigma_b \le \Delta t ,
  \label{eq:bus_entry}
\end{equation}
with $\hat{\tau}$ the tracker's estimate. That is a sensor delay, not a
distribution over journey times, and it is \emph{not} scaled by the dispersion
budget below: $\Gamma$ prices car counting, and bus trajectory is not part of it.
A model that instead drew bus arrivals from a headway distribution would be a
different model of the world from the one evaluated here, and no result below
depends on it.

\paragraph{Cars: one generalised arrival bound.}
Let $\bar{A}_{i,m,v,t}\ge 0$ be the mean per-step arrival count on movement $m$ of
class $v$ at intersection $i$, and let $\sigma_{i,m,v}$ be any scale estimate
available for that count---the standard deviation of the residuals of the
estimator actually in use, or the inter-quartile range of the same residuals
rescaled to a standard deviation. The program is given the bound
\begin{equation}
  A^{\max}_{i,m,v,t}
  \;=\;
  \bigl(1+\Gamma\,\delta_{i,m,v}\bigr)\,\bar{A}_{i,m,v,t},
  \qquad
  \delta_{i,m,v}=\frac{\sigma_{i,m,v}}{\bar{A}_{i,m,v,t}},
  \quad \Gamma\ge 0,
  \label{eq:arr_bound}
\end{equation}
and uses $A^{\max}$ wherever an arrival count appears: the integer
$n_{i,m,v,t}$ of \cref{sec:milp} is set to $\lceil A^{\max}_{i,m,v,t}\rceil$, so
the conservation, storage and clock constraints are unchanged in form.
$\delta_{i,m,v}$ is measured on the window the estimator reports on---a
60\,s detector count, not the 1\,s program step---and applied to the per-step
mean as a ratio. The distinction is not cosmetic: pure counting noise on a 1\,s
step at $900$\,veh/h gives $\delta\approx 2$, so $\Gamma=1$ would more than
double every arrival, whereas the same flow measured on a 60\,s count gives
$\delta\approx0.26$, so $\Gamma=1$ adds $26\%$ and $\Gamma=2$ adds $52\%$.
Relative dispersion is a property of the aggregation window, so the window is
part of the instance description. $\Gamma$ is a single scalar budget on relative
dispersion, not a fitted distribution: $\Gamma=0$ recovers the deterministic
program, $\Gamma=1$ admits a one-standard-deviation excursion on every movement
simultaneously, and $\Gamma$ belongs to the instance rather than to the model: an
instance is written $\mathcal{P}(\Gamma)$, and its uncertainty level is part of
the specification. No reported run instantiates the program, so no run carries a
$\Gamma$; \cref{sec:results} states what the runs do instead.

\paragraph{The null observation is a constraint, not a number.}
A detector that reports no vehicles is reporting something stronger than a low
flow: it is reporting that the queue is empty. The program carries that as a
linear cut,
\begin{equation}
  Q_{i,m,v,t+1} \;\le\; K_{i,m}\;\hat{o}^{\text{null}}_{i,m,t},
  \qquad \hat{o}^{\text{null}}_{i,m,t}\in\{0,1\},
  \label{eq:null_cut}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{o}^{\text{null}}_{i,m,t}=1$ unless the detector covering movement $m$
observed no vehicle during step $t$. The indicator is data read from the detector
record, so the cut adds no variable; it reuses the storage bound already in the
model and removes an entire region of the feasible set: with
$\hat{o}^{\text{null}}=0$ the queue is zero exactly. Three qualifications keep it
honest, and each of them comes from the data rather than from the algebra.
First, the cut is applied only where the detector is \emph{proven live}: a group
that fails to return, or returns the API's invalid-identifier code, is a dead
sensor and yields no cut---an unmeasured approach is not an empty one, and
conflating the two is the failure mode that makes an empty network and a broken
detector look identical. Second, it is exact only where the detector sits at the
stop line or covers the whole storage of the movement; a detector part-way along
the approach bounds the queue only up to the unobserved stretch, and is used as a
bound there. Third, it is one-sided: an observation of emptiness is used, an
observation of presence is not, because presence tells us only that
$Q\ge1$ and a one-vehicle detector reading cannot price a queue.
The controller encodes the same distinction---its empty-phase rule truncates a
phase only when the measured queue is at the threshold, and its detector
diagnostic separates ``no vehicles'' from ``no data''---so the program and the
implementation agree about what it means for the system to hold no cars.

\paragraph{Why this keeps the program linear.}
$A^{\max}$ and $\hat{o}^{\text{null}}$ are numbers, computed before the solve from
statistics of the estimator and from the detector record. Substituting them into
the conservation, storage and clock constraints moves right-hand sides only: no
variable is added, no product of variables appears, and no constraint is replaced
by its chance-constrained or scenario analogue. The program is therefore exactly
as linear and as integer as \cref{sec:milp} states, and increasing $\Gamma$
cannot change its structure---only its feasibility margin. That is the sense in
which the formulation is \emph{robust by bound} rather than stochastic: the
uncertainty is priced once, outside the model, and carried inside it as data,
while what the detectors actually saw is imposed as a hard cut.

