\subsection{How the Program Changes as Knowledge Arrives}
\label{sec:knowledge}

The program is not posed on a fixed input set. Every datum in it is either an
observation or an estimator output, and both arrive over time, so what the
controller faces is a family of instances,
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{P}\bigl(\Gamma;\ \mathcal{D}_t\bigr),
  \qquad
  \mathcal{D}_t=\bigl(\hat{X}_t,\ \{n_{i,m,v,\cdot}\},\ \{\tau_{b,i}\},\
  \{\hat{o}^{\text{null}}_{i,m,\cdot}\}\bigr),
  \label{eq:prog_family}
\end{equation}
one per knowledge state. What changes between instances is the datum, never the
model: the rows and columns of \cref{sec:milp} are the same in every instance and
only right-hand sides and the arrival sequence move. A null observation is
carried by setting its indicator to zero, so even knowledge \emph{lost}---a
detector failing---leaves the row in place and merely vacuous. That is the sense
in which the formulation is information-paced without being stochastic: knowledge
enters by rewriting numbers, so the program can be re-posed as often as
observations arrive, at no structural cost.

