\paragraph{Two things this does not license.}
First, a datum change is not a tightening. Replacing an arrival mean by zero, or
an indicator by zero, changes a right-hand side, so the two instances are
different programs; no inclusion between their feasible sets is claimed, and none
should be inferred from the word ``knowledge''. What is claimed is exact and
weaker: for a fixed datum set the program is the program of
\cref{sec:milp}, and across datum sets the difference is confined to the numbers
in \cref{tab:knowledge}. Second, the objective is a prediction on
$\mathcal{D}_t$, not a measurement: a better-informed instance does not have a
lower objective value, it has a better-supported one. The two must not be
compared as if a drop in $Z^{*}$ were an improvement in control.

\paragraph{Worked illustration: one approach, three knowledge states.}
Take one movement, a cycle of $60$\,s with $40$\,s of red, saturation
$1800$\,veh/h ($0.5$\,veh/s) and mean demand $900$\,veh/h ($0.25$\,veh/s); a
60\,s detector count of $15$ vehicles has $\sigma\approx3.9$, so
$\delta\approx0.26$.
In $\mathcal{D}^{(1)}$ only the mean is known ($\Gamma=0$): $A^{\max}=0.25$\,veh/s and the
queue at the end of red is $10$. In $\mathcal{D}^{(2)}$ the dispersion is known
and $\Gamma=1$, so $A^{\max}=0.315$\,veh/s and the same red builds $12.6$---a plan
sized for two and a half more vehicles, from the identical model. In
$\mathcal{D}^{(3)}$ the detector reports nothing at the start of the step, so
$n=0$ and \cref{eq:null_cut} pins the queue to zero: the delay term for that
movement vanishes and the storage constraint is slack. A $10$\,s green extension
serves $5$ vehicles at saturation, so its charged car cost is five vehicles'
waiting in $\mathcal{D}^{(1)}$ and $\mathcal{D}^{(2)}$ and zero in
$\mathcal{D}^{(3)}$---the cross-street charge is unchanged and still applies---and
an action that fails the cost veto in the first two states passes it in the third
with no guard threshold altered. That is the change the formulation is built to
show: same rows, same columns, different numbers, different decision.

\subsection{One junction, then the corridor: an exact linear composition}
\label{sec:isolated_joint}

\paragraph{The isolated instance.}
Restrict the index set to a single junction and drop $\Esp$, and with it the two
conservation rows (\cref{eq:cons_link,eq:cons_phase}). What remains is a complete
instance of the same model---gate exclusivity, minimum green, intergreen, the
green and cycle bounds, the queue/discharge/storage block, and the null
cut---with the arrival run $A_{i,m,v,\cdot}$ exogenous. Under uncertainty that is
the whole story at an isolated junction: the arrival datum is the only channel
through which an unknown enters, so the instance is fixed by two numbers per
movement, $A^{\max}$ (\cref{eq:arr_bound}) and $\hat{o}^{\text{null}}$
(\cref{eq:null_cut}), plus the measured bus clock, and it carries $|\Psp_i|\,|
\Tsp|$ binaries. Nothing else in it is discrete, and nothing in it is non-linear.

\paragraph{What connecting junctions adds, and what it does not.}
Exactly one datum changes. At an isolated junction $A_{i,m,v,t}$ is given; in the
corridor it is the upstream discharge, carried across the link by the two
conservation rows of \cref{eq:cons_link,eq:cons_phase}. Both are affine in the
phase, queue and discharge variables: $\gamma^{m}_{ij}\in\{0,1\}$ is fixed
geometry and $\tau_{ij}$ is a fixed travel time in steps, so every coefficient
that multiplies a variable is a constant, and the lag is an index shift rather
than a term. No other row of the model mentions a second junction.

\paragraph{The joint program is one MILP, and it is block-angular.}
Stacking the $N$ junction blocks and appending the coupling strip gives
\begin{equation}
  A(\mathcal{D}_t)\,u \;\le\; b(\Gamma;\mathcal{D}_t),
  \qquad
  A=\left[\begin{array}{@{}cccc@{}}
    A_1 & & & \\
     & A_2 & & \\
     & & \ddots & \\
     & & & A_N \\[2pt]
    C_1 & C_2 & \cdots & C_N
  \end{array}\right],
  \label{eq:block_angular}
\end{equation}
where $u=(u_1,\dots,u_N)$ collects each junction's own variables, $A_i$ is its
local constraint matrix with right-hand side $b_i$, and the single bottom strip
$C$ holds \cref{eq:cons_link,eq:cons_phase}. The diagonal is diagonal because no
local row mentions another junction; the strip is the only place two index sets
meet. Uncertainty and knowledge enter the diagonal blocks only, through
$b_i(\Gamma;\mathcal{D}_t)$---which is what makes the model both linear and
separable in its data even though the decisions are not separable.

\begin{proposition}[Exact composition]
\label{prop:compose}
Let $F_i$ be the feasible set of the isolated instance at junction $i$ with its
arrival run set to $A^{\max}$ and its null indicator fixed, and let
$\mathcal{C}$ be the affine set defined by \cref{eq:cons_link,eq:cons_phase}.
Then the feasible set of the corridor program is
\begin{equation}
  F \;=\; \bigl\{\,u \;:\; u_i \in F_i \ \ \forall i \,\bigr\} \;\cap\; \mathcal{C},
  \label{eq:compose}
\end{equation}
exactly, with no relaxation and no approximation.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Every row of the corridor program is either a row of exactly one local system
$A_i u_i\le b_i$ or a row of the conservation strip. A point therefore satisfies
the corridor program if and only if it satisfies every local system and the
strip, which is \cref{eq:compose}. The claim is bookkeeping, but it is the
bookkeeping that licenses calling the composition exact: nothing new is added
when junctions are connected except two affine rows, and nothing is dropped.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
\label{cor:decomp}
$\sum_i Z_i^{\star} \le Z^{\star}_{\text{corridor}}$, where $Z_i^{\star}$
minimises the isolated instance at $i$.
\end{corollary}

The corollary is the honest half of the statement. The \emph{formulation}
composes exactly, but the \emph{optimisation} does not: relaxing the strip by
minimising each block on its own can only do better, so per-junction results are a
lower bound on the corridor optimum, and the gap is precisely the value of
coordination. A decomposition algorithm---Benders, or a Lagrangian dual on the
strip---would close the gap by pricing it; the block-angular structure of
\cref{eq:block_angular} is what such an algorithm exploits. None is run here, and
the online methods of \cref{sec:solutions} do not solve either program.

\paragraph{What the gap would cost on Kelvin Grove, and why the reported numbers
cannot price it.}
\Cref{cor:decomp} is a bound, not an estimator, and the natural next
question---what coordination is worth on the Kelvin Grove corridor---cannot be
answered from \cref{tab:kg_results}. Those entries are realised costs of
schedules produced by the online methods, not optima of any instance:
$Z_i^{\star}$ is the best cost attainable at $i$ when its arrivals are exogenous
and only its own constraints apply, and no run computes it, because no instance
is solved (\cref{sec:milp_status}). The gap therefore has no measured value
here, and none is quoted; a percentage would be invented. For scale only, the
corridor's total passenger delay at M0 is $692$\,pax$\cdot$h over twelve
junctions, an average of $57.7$\,pax$\cdot$h per junction, and a coordination
regret is a per-junction quantity of that order---but its value is unmeasured.

Two properties do transfer, and they say what a measurement would have to do.
First, the gap decomposes into local regrets: the corridor optimum $u^{\star}$ is
locally feasible by \cref{prop:compose}, so
\begin{equation}
  G \;=\; Z^{\star}_{\text{corr}}-\sum_i Z_i^{\star}
    \;=\; \sum_i\bigl[\,Z_i(u_i^{\star}) - Z_i^{\star}\,\bigr],
  \label{eq:regret}
\end{equation}
and every bracket is non-negative. Coordination costs each junction exactly its
distance from its own best schedule, so the gap is reportable per junction
rather than only as a corridor total. Second, the gap vanishes when the strip is
not binding: if no link reaches its storage bound in the corridor solution, the
conservation rows are slack, the local optima are mutually consistent, and
$G=0$. Kelvin Grove is near-saturated, so that condition plausibly fails---but by
how much is precisely the unmeasured quantity.

The cheapest measurement that would price it is an experiment rather than a
re-reading of the tables: solve the isolated instance once per junction on the
arrivals measured in a single run, then evaluate each junction's realised cost
under the corridor's schedule. The sum of those differences is $G$ for that
run---twelve small MILPs and one pass per seed.

\paragraph{Where the linearity comes from, and where it would stop.}
The composition is exact and linear because $\tau_{ij}$ and $\gamma^{m}_{ij}$ are
data. Let $\tau_{ij}$ depend on flow through a speed--density relation, or on the
signal through a discharge rate that varies with the remaining green, and the
coupling row acquires a product of a variable with a variable; the joint program
then stops being a MILP, and the non-linear delay models of
\cref{sec:dynamics} would have to move inside it. The fixed lag is not a
convenience---it is the assumption that buys \cref{prop:compose}, and it is the
same assumption the progression term already makes.

\paragraph{Knowledge travels on the same rows.}
A null observation at $i$ fixes $Q_{i,\cdot,t}=0$ (\cref{eq:null_cut}), and the
discharge it permits is carried by \cref{eq:cons_link} into $j$'s arrival datum at
$t+\tau_{ij}$. The corridor's knowledge state is therefore the vector of local
observations transported by the same affine rows that carry the flow, so what one
junction's detector implies for the next is stated exactly rather than
heuristically---and, because the transport is affine, a null observation upstream
can tighten the downstream instance without ever introducing a probability.

\subsection{Computational Status}
\label{sec:milp_status}

The word ``exact'' refers to the formulation, not to a solved benchmark. No
MILP objective value, optimality gap, solution time, node count, or
branch-and-bound statistic is reported, because the full-horizon program was
not solved to optimality on the Kelvin Grove corridor in this study, and no
number from it is used to rank a controller. All percentage changes in the
Abstract and in \cref{sec:results} are microsimulation outcomes (Aimsun Next,
\texttt{champion\_kg.csv}) measured against the \texttt{NO\_TSP} fixed-time
baseline, not against an MILP optimum.

\paragraph{Computational scale.}
At $\Delta t = 1$\,s the scored horizon is $T = 4500$ steps (1.25\,h) over
$|\Isp| = 12$ intersections with $|\Psp_i| \approx 4$ phases each. The
time-expanded model therefore has approximately
$|\Isp|\,|\Psp_i|\,T \approx 2.2\times10^5$ binary phase-state variables
$x_{i,p,t}$, of order $10^5$ integer queue, discharge, delay,
progression-overlap, and bus-clock variables, and of order $10^6$ constraints.
These are order-of-magnitude counts from the instance dimensions, not solver
statistics. A branch-and-bound solve at this scale is not compatible with the
one-second decision cadence of the controller, nor with the compute budget of
this study, and no attempt is made here to obtain an optimality certificate.
The program is therefore used as a reference formulation, not as an online
solver.

\paragraph{Role of the formulation.}
The program is the normative definition of the control problem---what should be
done at every intersection and second under perfect prediction---and the
reference against which the online methods are evaluated. The equivalent
Bellman formulation in \cref{sec:markovian} is a representation of the same
program, not a second model; the online methods in \cref{sec:solutions}
approximate it event by event. Its value is definitional and comparative rather
than numerical: one model, one criterion, and one set of constraints against
which the six evaluated methods are compared on the common microsimulation
criterion.

\subsection{Model Boundary}
\label{sec:milp_boundary}

The program is an \emph{exact} MILP of a \emph{simplified} model: a
point-queue network with finite link storage, integer clocks, and a fixed
cycle envelope.  It is not an exact optimisation of the LWR kinematic-wave,
shockwave, or cell-transmission dynamics described in \cref{sec:dynamics};
those are background \emph{estimation} models used by the online layer
(\cref{sec:solutions}) to predict queues and ETAs, and they enter this
program only through the exogenous arrivals $n_{i,m,v,t}$ and the storage
bounds $K_{i,m}$.  Likewise, the program does not model moving bottlenecks
or spillback beyond the finite-storage block (\cref{eq:storage_cap,eq:discharge_space}).
Stating the boundary precisely is what makes the word ``exact'' honest: the
program is exact with respect to the model it defines, and the model is
deliberately simpler than the traffic it approximates.

\subsection{What the program fixes exactly, and what an algorithm must supply}
\label{sec:exact_vs_algo}

The program is exact with respect to its own model, and the model is deliberately
narrow. It is therefore worth stating once, explicitly, where the boundary lies:
which quantities the MILP \emph{controls}, and which are necessarily left to a
solution algorithm because they are non-linear, stochastic, or combinatorial at a
scale the program does not attempt. \cref{tab:exact_algo} is that boundary, and it
governs how every result in Sec.~\ref{sec:results} should be read.

