The Logan corridor is instrumented with the same three detector classes as
Kelvin Grove (approach, exit, and side-street), and is evaluated under the
\emph{identical} strategy-comparison protocol of \cref{sec:batch}: the same six
ladder methods scored at demand scalar~1.0 over seeds 300--700 (learners
pre-trained on seeds 800--1100 and frozen), on the same 1.25\,h simulation.
The ladder ran in two sessions (seeds 300--500, then 500--700 on a later
build); the repeated seed 500 reproduces the published rows exactly on every
outcome column, which validates the merge.
Corridor-specific tunings that differ
from Kelvin Grove -- a higher cost-veto ratio and a tighter congestion phase on
the more saturated cross streets -- are applied per arm with the values used in
the batch. Two corridor-structural fixes are required for the network
cost terms to apply corridor-wide on Logan: the downstream cascade cost must be
wired to \emph{every} route junction, and the measured cross-approach flow must
fall back to the model-topology section length so that virtual/entry side
sections report their scanned flow rather than a demand-rated warm-up seed.

The Logan results are reported in \cref{sec:results} next to the Kelvin Grove
ladder, on the same objective and the same metrics, so the two corridors can be
read against each other directly.

\subsection{Batch Experiment Design}
\label{sec:batch}

The evaluation is one scripted Aimsun Next batch protocol, run on both
corridors. Every strategy is scored on the same objective, the served-passenger
rate per delay-hour (higher is better); the headline tables below report it
equivalently as total passenger delay (pax\,hrs, lower is better), which the
objective normalises. One factor varies across cells -- the strategy on the
adaptive base -- and everything else is held fixed (\cref{tab:batch_design}).

