\paragraph{Dispersion budget: none is applied to any reported run.}
No result in this section solves the program, so no run has a $\Gamma$ to report:
$\Gamma$ specifies an instance, and the instances here are empty. The bus side is
not affected either way: bus clocks are measured at the step, so no dispersion
budget applies to them. What the runs
do instead is realise the uncertainty that the bound would have priced---Aimsun
draws each seed's arrivals, and every controller reads the measured per-step
counts, that is the realised $A$, never $\bar{A}$ and never $A^{\max}$---so the
numbers below are realised-scenario outcomes, not the outcome of a robust program
at any $\Gamma$. Reading them as ``$\Gamma=0$'' would be wrong in the other
direction: $\Gamma=0$ is a statement about a bound that was never imposed, not a
claim that the arrival process was treated as deterministic. The bound matters to
the design question of \cref{sec:stochastic}, and the instance that would test it
is a future solve, not a re-reading of these tables.

