\textbf{Action-rate/delay monotonicity survives correct gating.} Across all twelve
rungs the five-seed delay change is an almost monotone function of the committed
action rate (\cref{fig:kg_monotonic}): every rung that commits $\lesssim 1$
action/run improves on fixed-time by $4$--$5\%$ by deferring to the base, and
every rung that commits $\gtrsim 5$ actions/run worsens it by $8$--$10\%$, with no
acting-heavy rung beating a near-inert one. The gating spectrum within one solver
isolates the mechanism. The ungated Nash game (G1) acts $46$ times per run and is
the worst rung on the corridor ($+9.6\%$). A degree-of-saturation admission gate
(G2, G3) silences it to $\approx\!0$ actions --- but only as an artefact: the DS
estimator it reads is inflated $\sim\!18\times$ by summing a movement's detector
loops in series, so it reports over-saturation ($\mathrm{DS}\!\approx\!3.7$ where
the true value is $\approx\!0.3$) and phantom-holds two-thirds of all solves; its
``win'' ($-4.0$ to $-4.6\%$) is the control standing down, not the game choosing
well. Replacing that upper bound with the cell-state jam fraction --- a direct
vehicle-count-over-storage occupancy that is immune to the detector inflation ---
restricting admission to the bus-beneficial, spillback-priced set, and dropping the
car-queue floor so a bus can still be served in light traffic, makes the gate
physically correct. The game, now genuinely permitted to act ($5.4$ bus-helping
commits/run, G4/G5), returns to $+8\%$, beside the heavily-acting bus-first game
(M4, \cref{tab:kg_results}). Correct gating therefore \emph{relocates} the arm
along the action--delay curve --- from the inert left edge to the action rate its
rule admits --- rather than bending the curve downward: making the admission rule
sound and transit-targeted does not create a regime in which acting pays. The
residual cost of each bus grant on an already-coordinated corridor is a downstream
progression break that the single-junction, $60$\,s cooperative payoff cannot
price, so the only priority layers that beat the adaptive base remain the ones that
mostly defer to it. We verified that this is a horizon limit and not a
bookkeeping omission: the bus-first and cooperative games already charge the full
cross- and side-street cost of each grant inside their candidate payoff, and
forcing an additional measured side-delay term onto every decision leaves the
five-seed delay byte-identical---the residual damage lies downstream of any cost a
single-junction evaluator can observe. This is the strongest form of the paper's central result: it
holds not just for the heuristic rungs but for a cooperative game whose admission
rule has been audited down to the occupancy signal it reads.

\textbf{Out-of-sample corroboration on a second corridor.} We replayed the same
two-class-CTM game family on the twenty-three-intersection Logan Road arterial, a
more heavily saturated network than Kelvin Grove. The pattern is reproduced, and
the mechanism is visible in the action rate. Priced on the bare single-junction
$60$\,s CTM horizon, the welfare games over-commit---roughly $110$ to $140$
priority actions per run---and fall $4$ to $8\%$ below the fixed-time baseline on
the passenger-efficiency objective (passengers served per delay-hour), the heaviest
actor (asymmetric Nash) losing the most. Re-pricing each grant with the physically
correct payoff---bounding admission by the cell-state jam fraction rather than the
detector-derived saturation estimate, and throttling each approach's stop-line
discharge by its downstream receiving link's free space---suppresses roughly $85\%$
of those actions (to about $20$ per run) \emph{without any explicit veto}: the
net-harmful grants simply stop clearing the candidate payoff. The five-seed
objective recovers from $-6.7\%$ to $-1.3\%$ for asymmetric Nash and lands between
break-even and $-3\%$ for the remaining cooperative variants. Correct pricing thus
moves each Logan arm back up the same action--delay curve by withholding the grants
whose downstream cost it can now see; but on a saturated arterial the best-priced
priority layer only reaches the fixed-time frontier, it does not pass it---the
adaptive base again carries the corridor.

