\subsection{Where the Gain Occurs: Junction-Level Telemetry}
\label{sec:kg_telemetry}

The tables above average over the corridor and over the whole run. The
controller's own telemetry---per-junction queues and cumulative passenger delay
every 60\,s, plus one record per applied action---locates the effect in space,
in time, and in the state that invited it. We read it from the scored runs
themselves: five seeds per rung, with the M1 row taken from the companion batch
exactly as in \cref{tab:kg_results}. The reconciliation is close: summing the
per-junction cumulative delay reproduces the reported corridor total to within
$1.1\%$ for every rung (ratios $0.99$--$1.01$), so the attribution below is the
same measurement, not a parallel model. The queue field used here is the total
queue (main plus side) from the scored ladder, not the mainline-only snapshot of
the corridor-setup figure, which came from an earlier campaign.

\paragraph{Where the delay moves.} \cref{fig:kg_where}(a) attributes the
five-seed change in passenger delay to each junction in route order. The
adaptive base and the cooperative game share one profile: nearly all of the
saving is made at J5 (Aimsun 39593), at $-20.8$ and $-20.4$\,pax$\cdot$h of a
total change of $-28.6$ and $-22.7$\,h---73\% and 90\% of the whole effect---with
J2, J4 and J11 also negative, and J3 ($+7.2$ and $+9.5$) and the downstream
boundary J12 ($+5.1$ and $+5.2$) paying for it. The bus-first game is the mirror image:
$+31.1$\,h at J3, $+23.1$ at J2 and $+9.0$ at J1, i.e.\ $+63$\,h across the
three upstream junctions, partly offset by $-9.5$ at J5 and $-6.8$ at J11, for a
net $+48.9$\,h. By class, M1 saves 8.2\,h of bus delay and 20.4\,h of car delay,
M5 saves 6.9 and 15.8, and M4 \emph{adds} 8.5\,h of bus delay and 38.5\,h of car
delay (the remainder in each total is truck delay). The bus-first game does buy
relief at the junction it serves; it pays for
it with a car queue two junctions upstream. At corridor level the loss is a
spillback, not a redistribution between classes.

\paragraph{When the gain appears.} \cref{fig:kg_where}(b) adds the time
dimension. The adaptive plan is not a free lunch at the start: against
fixed-time it is $+7$\,pax$\cdot$h \emph{worse} at 30\,min, crosses zero at
about 44\,min, and reaches $-18$ at 60\,min and $-29$ at 75\,min; M5 follows the
same curve. M4 is within about 1\,pax$\cdot$h until 15\,min and then diverges:
$+24$ at 45\,min, $+35$ at 60 and $+49$ at 75. The middle rungs follow the same
sign change later and more weakly: M2 is $-3.2$ at 60\,min and $-7.6$ at the
end, while M3 recovers to $-7.9$ at 60\,min before turning back up to $-3.7$ as
its seed-500 realisation diverges---the timing signature of the tail discussed
above. The gain is therefore
back-loaded. It is
what the corridor recovers once the re-optimised plan has been in force long
enough to clear standing queues, and a 30-min evaluation would have concluded
that the base does nothing and that M4 costs nothing.

