The Kelvin Grove ladder was repeated on the 23-junction Logan Road corridor
with the same six rungs, the same adaptive DSW base, and the same guard
structure (\cref{tab:methods}); the Logan arms carry the \texttt{\_LOGAN}
suffix, with the corridor's higher cost-veto ratio and tighter congestion
phase. Logan is the harder test: the same 1.25\,h peak is spread over nearly
twice as many signals, and its cross streets carry substantially heavier and
more variable traffic, so the fixed-time baseline delay is more than twice as
large (1{,}519 vs.\ 692\,pax\,h). The batch runs over the same five seeds as
Kelvin Grove (300--700), in two sessions: seeds 300--500 on the published
build, then 500--700 on a later build, with the repeated seed 500 reproducing
the published rows exactly on every outcome column, so the merged ladder is one
protocol. Only differences well outside the seed spread are read as effects.

