\textbf{Key findings (Logan Road corridor, six-method ladder, five-seed means -- statistical limits in Discussion):}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{The adaptive base changes nothing detectable on Logan.} Against
fixed-time, M1's total passenger delay is $+0.4\%$ (1{,}525 vs.\ 1{,}519\,pax\,h)
and its throughput $+1.8\%$ -- but the baseline guard flags seed 300 (the first
replication of its session, throughput $11\%$ below the median), and without
that seed the same quantities are $-0.7\%$ and $-0.4\%$. Every small positive
on Logan is an artefact of one starved baseline replication, not a gain: on a
corridor already at capacity there is no headroom for the plan to convert, which
is the saturating counterpart of the Kelvin Grove result. The reference point
for a priority layer is therefore an adaptive plan that has already absorbed
the corridor's slack.
\item \textbf{On the saturated corridor every priority layer except the
bus-first game is inert, and the outcome ordering survives the change of
corridor.} The cooperative game (M5, $+0.2\%$ total delay), the learned
priority head (M2, $-0.0\%$), and the guarded CellQLearn (M3, $+0.3\%$) all lie
within seed noise of the base: between them the three layers commit six actions
across the five seeds,
and none at all in most of them, so they
essentially stand down. The ranking is the
same as on Kelvin Grove (M5 $\approx$ M2 $\approx$ M3 $\approx$ M1, with M4
last), and the gaps between them collapse toward zero for the reason identified
there: with the
cross phases at or near their minimum green the recoverable headroom
$\hat{R}_{i,t}$ of \cref{eq:recoverable} is close to zero, so a grant cannot be
repaid and the guards are right to decline it. Under the corridor's disclosed
tuning the ladder produces ``do not intervene'' on Logan through the same guard
and admission structure, which is the behaviour a corridor operator would want.
\item \textbf{The bus-first game is the only arm that moves the corridor, and
it moves it the wrong way.} M4 commits 6.4 green extensions, 6.4 insertions,
and 3.0 early reds per run (103.6\,s of added green, more than an order of
magnitude
more than any other rung), and raises total delay $4.9\%$ while lowering the
objective $3.2\%$. Buses are not compensated: bus delay is the highest of the
ladder (7.8\,s/pax), bus travel time is the slowest (54.0\,s), car delay rises
to 32.3\,s/pax, and corridor speed falls to 13.7\,km/h. This is a lose--lose
outcome, not a bus-versus-car trade, and it is the same offset-breaking failure
the Kelvin Grove recoverability finding predicts.
\item \textbf{Cross-corridor comparison: the proportional penalty is smaller on
Logan, the absolute penalty is larger.} M4 costs $+4.9\%$ of total delay on
Logan against $+7.9\%$ on Kelvin Grove, yet that is $+75$\,pax\,h against
$+55$\,pax\,h, because the Logan baseline already carries 2.2 times the delay
(1{,}519 vs.\ 692\,pax\,h). The direction and the ordering of the ladder are
preserved across two corridors with very different saturation; the magnitude is
set by how much delay the baseline already holds. At five seeds the
$\pm0.4\%$ clustering among the top four rungs persists, so only M4's effect is
large enough to read as more than seed noise, and we make no claim that the
ordering among M1--M3 and M5 is resolved by this experiment.
\end{enumerate}

