\section{Discussion}

Four findings emerge from the two corridor experiments:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{On a well-timed, car-dominated corridor already running an adaptive
plan, a per-junction bus-priority layer is hard to make net-beneficial (Kelvin
Grove).} The DSW
adaptive base (M1) alone cuts total passenger delay $4.9\%$; of the four
bus-priority layers stacked on it, only the cooperative game (M5) matches the base,
two are between the base and neutral (M2, M3), and the bus-first game (M4)
gridlocks the corridor by $+7.9\%$, driven by cross-street car delay (34.3 vs.\
33.2\,s/pax). The bottleneck is not the search strategy but the \emph{cost model}
and the reference point: once the plan already adapts to demand, a decision that
prices only the local cross approach cannot see the downstream gridlock its
released platoon will cause, and has little headroom left to improve.
\item \textbf{Pricing the downstream network cost is what keeps priority from
becoming a lottery.} The spread across the four priority layers on the identical
base on Kelvin Grove ($-4.0\%$ to $+7.9\%$ total delay) tracks how tightly each
prices the
cross/downstream cost: the only heavy actor (M4) commits where that cost is high
and cascades, while the deferential arms (M5, M2, M3) hold the coordinated plan.
The junction ledger makes the mechanism concrete (Sec.~\ref{sec:kg_telemetry}):
the winning layers take 73--90\% of their saving at a single junction (J5) and
grant only when the side street is well below its median flow, whereas the
bus-first game grants at the median decision state and its loss reappears as
$+63$\,pax$\cdot$h of extra delay at the three junctions upstream of where it
acts.
Even with the total-passenger net-benefit test and projected-saturation cascade
(\cref{sec:gate_cascade}), a residual survives, and it has two distinct sources.
The one large tail---M3's seed-500 blow-up of $+24\%$---is a split-solver
occupancy artefact at J5 rather than a reward error: a loop-independent
occupancy floor recovers most of it (863 to 761\,pax$\cdot$h on that seed), but
costs 8--27\,pax$\cdot$h on the other seeds and does not help the other rungs
(\cref{sec:results}). The smaller, pervasive residual is the analytic-cost
problem proper: the per-action benefit is predicted on a local cost and scored
on measured delay, so an action that looks good locally still goes wrong on some
seeds. This is the practical counterpart of the system-optimal plan of
\citet{Abdelfatah1998SystemOptimal}: optimising timing over a whole network is
only as good as the cost function being minimised, and a local, analytically
priced cost makes a globally harmful action look locally attractive.
\item \textbf{Bus-favourable and corridor-favourable are distinct objectives.}
The lowest bus delays ($10.8$--$10.9$\,s/pax) belong to the arms that mostly defer
to the coordinated plan (M5, M1), which reach them \emph{without} a car penalty;
the bus-first game (M4) chases bus delay directly and loads the cross street enough
to be net-negative on total passenger delay. A corridor operator choosing a
controller must decide which of the two it is optimising; the arms that win on
total delay do so by restraint, not by maximising bus priority.
\item \textbf{Saturation changes the size of the effect, not its direction.}
The ladder ranks the same way on both corridors: the deferential layers
(M1, M5, M2, M3) cluster together and the bus-first game (M4) is last. What
changes is how the gain is expressed and how large the loss is. On Kelvin
Grove the base turns its headroom into $4.9\%$ less delay; on Logan, where
fixed-time is already at capacity, the same headroom buys nothing detectable --
every small Logan gain reverses sign when the flagged baseline seed is
excluded. M4's penalty is proportionally smaller on
Logan
($+4.9\%$ vs.\ $+7.9\%$) but absolutely larger ($+75$ vs.\ $+55$\,pax\,h),
because the Logan baseline already carries 2.2 times the delay
(1{,}519 vs.\ 692\,pax\,h). The damage a priority layer can do is bounded by
the delay available to damage, so a percentage-on-baseline comparison across
corridors is misleading on its own.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Role of the full formulation.}
The spread across the bus-priority layers on the identical adaptive base
($-4.0\%$ to $+7.9\%$ total delay) is the largest controllable effect in
\cref{tab:kg_results}, while the formulation also specifies signal switching,
queues, offsets, bus clocks, and the rules the online layer applies (R1--R11,
\cref{sec:rule_tsp}). Each block is necessary because it prices one side of the
decision; the table maps each block to its online counterpart.
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.9cm}p{2.7cm}p{10.9cm}@{}}
\hline
MILP block & Online counterpart & Necessity evidence in this paper \\
\hline
Queue/discharge + queue-waiting sums (\cref{eq:mv_dcap,eq:mv_q}, \ref{eq:mv_q}--\ref{eq:mv_waitsum}) & Measured-side floor + CTM/LWR benefit estimate & Without it, bus benefit and car cost are incommensurable; the permissive rule layer ($+13.3\%$) and the bus-first game M4 ($+7.9\%$) price neither side correctly. \\
Bus clocks + schedule caps (\cref{eq:arr_prop,eq:sched_cap}, $Z_3$) & ETA window, $\hat{\sigma}^+$, takeover, MIN\_GAIN & Removes grants to on-time buses (conditional-priority principle); unguarded policies grant where no lateness exists. \\
Progression overlap + bus coupling (\cref{eq:band_min,eq:prog_bus}) & Projected-saturation cascade (Sec.~\ref{sec:gate_cascade}) & The cascade is the online projection of R4/progression: the heavy-acting M4 commits where downstream cost is high and cascades, while the deferential arms hold (Tables~\ref{tab:kg_results}--\ref{tab:kg_tsp}). \\
R1 balance (\cref{eq:rule_balance}) & Net-benefit test (\ref{eq:net_pick}) & Prices the bus-vs-cross trade so the winning layers defer to the base rather than cascade. \\
R2--R3/R5--R7 (phase, cooldown, horizon, legality) & $\hat{\Ucal}$ (\cref{eq:online_det,eq:online_phase}), A0 & Prevents wrong-phase GE, oscillation, and illegal splits. \\
$Z_4$ cap (\cref{eq:Z4}) & $Z_4$ cap in A0/selection layer & Shared veto (300\,veh\,s) that discards corridor-harmful survivors before the scalar $\arg\min$. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The admission rule decides, but the MILP defines what the rule compares.
Dropping the physical model (queues, clocks, offsets) leaves the rule with
nothing to price; dropping R1 leaves an accurate price unused. The minimal
deployable subset supported by this evaluation is therefore: (i) a bus-benefit
estimator with schedule conditioning, (ii) a measured cross-cost plus a
downstream-cascade term, and (iii) a hard net-benefit test with
phase/cooldown/spillback feasibility---R1+R4 plus clocks and queues. Phase
rotation and variable-phase skipping are not necessary on either corridor
(committed zero times on Logan, and not used on Kelvin Grove); the
bus-first game layer (M4) is retained as a benchmark and is the only rung that
underperforms the fixed-time baseline on either corridor, whereas its
cooperative counterpart (M5) is among the best.

\paragraph{Statistical limits and calibration.}
All headline numbers are means over eval seeds: five (300--700) on Kelvin
Grove and five (300--700) on Logan Road. The total-delay intervals are
collected in \cref{tab:seed_uncertainty}; no hypothesis test is reported, so no
claim of statistical significance is made. The intervals are wide and the paper
is read accordingly: on Kelvin Grove
the M1--M0 delay difference is 34\,pax$\cdot$h against intervals of
$\pm45$ and $\pm39$, and on Logan every adaptive interval overlaps the
fixed-time one, so ``best'' throughout means best seed-mean and the ordering is
a mean ordering, not a significance result. Calibration was held fixed and fair
across arms: identical state feed, identical guard protocol A0, identical eval
seeds per corridor, per-arm veto/gain parameters disclosed in \cref{tab:methods}.
The one underperforming arm (M4, $+7.9\%$ on Kelvin Grove and $+4.9\%$ on Logan)
received the same calibration budget; its failure is the finding, not a setup
artefact--\cref{tab:kg_tsp} and \cref{tab:logan_tsp} show it is the only arm
that intervenes heavily on either corridor, committing where the downstream
cost is high, which is exactly what a missing cascade term predicts.

\section{Conclusions and Recommendations}
\label{sec:conclusions}

This paper presented a proof-of-concept exact MILP formulation for corridor-level TSP (never solved) and a family of online heuristics evaluated on two Brisbane corridors, five random-seed replications each: the 12-intersection Kelvin Grove corridor and the 23-intersection Logan Road corridor.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{The adaptive plan base carries the corridor; the best bus-priority
layer only matches it.} On the Kelvin Grove Corridor the DSW adaptive base (M1)
gives the largest net improvement ($-4.9\%$ total, $-13\%$ bus, $-10\%$ car,
$+5.2\%$ throughput, five-seed mean; no significance test claimed), and of the
four priority layers stacked on it only the cooperative game (M5) matches it
($-4.0\%$ total). Both reach the lowest bus delays ($\approx$10.8\,s/pax) by
deferring to the coordinated plan rather than overriding it. On Logan Road the
base is indistinguishable from fixed-time on every headline metric (delay
$+0.4\%$, throughput $+1.8\%$, both reversing sign when the flagged baseline
seed is excluded), and the deferential layers cluster with it: on a corridor
already at capacity there is nothing for the plan to win.
\item \textbf{The network cost model, not the search strategy, is decisive.} On
the identical base, the bus-first game (M4) gridlocks the corridor ($+7.9\%$) and
the guarded CellQLearn (M3) blows up on one seed ($+24\%$, dragging its mean to
neutral), because a decision priced on the local cross approach with an analytic
cost cannot see the downstream gridlock its released platoon causes. The
net-benefit test and projected-saturation cascade (\cref{sec:gate_cascade}) shrink
this tail; grounding the reward in measured delay removes the residual.
\item \textbf{Transit priority wins by restraint, not by maximising bus green.}
The arms that improve total passenger delay are the ones that commit
$\lesssim$1 action per run and hold the coordinated plan; the only heavy actor is
also the only arm to raise total delay. Chasing bus delay directly (M4) loads the
cross street enough to go net-negative.
\item \textbf{Saturation changes the size of the effect, not its direction.}
The ladder ranks the same way on both corridors, and the bus-first game is last
on both. Its penalty is proportionally smaller on the saturated Logan corridor
($+4.9\%$ vs.\ $+7.9\%$ total delay) but absolutely larger ($+75$ vs.\
$+55$\,pax\,h), because the baseline it is measured against already carries
2.2 times the delay (1{,}519 vs.\ 692\,pax\,h). Percentage-on-baseline comparisons across corridors should
therefore be read together with the baseline delay in absolute terms.
\item \textbf{The exact MILP provides a normative formulation, not a solved benchmark} (Sec.~\ref{sec:milp_status}) and a common
framework for comparing statistical solution methods across network geometries
and demand levels.
\end{enumerate}

Future work: extending the downstream cascade beyond a single route and pricing
multi-approach spillback so the benefit generalises across demand; further
seeds on Logan Road, where the deferring arms remain within $0.4\%$ of the base
even at five seeds; and joint $Z_1$/$Z_4$
optimisation.

\end{linenumbers}
