\section{Solution Methods}
\label{sec:solutions}
% ==============================================================================

The exact program of \cref{sec:milp} is a whole-horizon,
integer-time traffic model.  The implementation does not solve it jointly:
at each decision epoch $t \in \Tsp^{\text{trig}}$--a bus detection, a timer
tick under \texttt{CONTINUOUS\_MONITOR\_MODE}, or an empty-phase event--a single
junction solves a single-action greedy subproblem from an \emph{estimated}
state $\hat{X}_t$ (measured flows/queues, Kalman ETA when a bus is present, live phase, remaining
time, POG, pressures), commits at integer-second resolution, and advances.  This section
therefore defines (i) the online guard set shared by every rung,
(ii) the shared selection layer, and
(iii) the priority layers added on top of the plan (\cref{tab:methods} maps
the five methods).  Online
guards, the POG tracker, the bus-detection triggers, and the conditional-priority
rule layer R1--R11 live only here. They never constrain the exact program: the
program of \cref{sec:milp} contains only what a signal setting can change,
and every mechanism that \emph{observes} the corridor --- detection, cooldown,
spillback, congestion, veto, Purdue coordination, and the rule layer
(Sec.~\ref{sec:rule_tsp}) --- is part of the solution algorithm, not of the
program it solves. The two therefore differ in kind, not in degree: the program
has no notion of \emph{right now}.
All online quantities are integers (seconds, steps, vehicles, pax\,s).
\cref{tab:method_map} maps every rung to its propose/commit branch,
and \cref{tab:state_space} with Fig.~\ref{fig:state_space} detail the estimated
state those branches observe; \cref{tab:methods} then gives the scored-run
parameters for the same methods.

\subsection{Candidate generation: the Webster split rule}
\label{app:webster}

The phase-rotation step (\cref{sec:phase_rot}) compares a small set of candidate
sequences $\mathcal{R}_i$.  A candidate is a \emph{sequence} of phases, not a
schedule, so each must be given a split before it can be scored at all.  The
split is supplied by the uniform-delay proxy of \citet{Webster1958Traffic}.
For cycle length $C_i$, total lost time $L_i$, measured arrival rate $q_{i,p}$
and saturation discharge $\mu_{i,p}$ on phase $p\in\mathcal{P}_i$, the
allocation solves
\begin{equation}
  \min_{g_{i,p,T}} \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}_i}
    \frac{\bigl(q_{i,p}\,C_i\bigr)^2}{2\,\mu_{i,p}\,g_{i,p,T}}
  \quad \text{subject to} \quad
  \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}_i} g_{i,p,T} = C_i - L_i,\quad
  g_{i,p,T} \ge G_i^{\min}.
  \label{eq:rot_green}
\end{equation}
The objective is strictly convex in $g$ on the positive orthant (each term has
second derivative $2a_p/g_{i,p,T}^{3}>0$), so \eqref{eq:rot_green} has a unique
solution.  With $a_p = (q_{i,p}C_i)^2/(2\mu_{i,p})$ and multiplier $\lambda$ on
the cycle budget, stationarity gives $-a_p/g_{i,p,T}^{2}+\lambda-\nu_p = 0$ with
$\nu_p\ge 0$ the multiplier of the lower bound, so
\begin{equation}
  g_{i,p,T}(\lambda) = \max\!\left\{G_i^{\min},\; \sqrt{a_p/\lambda}\right\},
  \qquad
  \sum_{p\in\mathcal{P}_i} g_{i,p,T}(\lambda) = C_i - L_i .
  \label{eq:webster_waterfill}
\end{equation}
This is a water-filling rule: without the bounds it collapses to
$g_{i,p,T}\propto\sqrt{a_p}\propto q_{i,p}/\sqrt{\mu_{i,p}}$, and with them the
single scalar $\lambda$ is recovered by bisection, since the left side of
\eqref{eq:webster_waterfill} is non-increasing in $\lambda$.  Because the bound
is a maximum, the phases below $G_i^{\min}$ are served at the bound and the
remainder is reallocated among the others \emph{at the new multiplier}---not by
rescaling the unconstrained solution.

The implementation uses a single clipping pass: it clips the unconstrained
split at $G_i^{\min}$ and renormalises the remainder proportionally, without
re-solving \eqref{eq:webster_waterfill} for $\lambda$.  The two coincide when no
bound binds and differ otherwise; the difference is confined to the split
\emph{proposed} for a candidate, never to feasibility, since every clip
respects $g_{i,p,T}\ge G_i^{\min}$ and the renormalisation restores the budget.

Two properties matter for how this is read.  First, \eqref{eq:rot_green} is a
one-cycle uniform-arrival proxy: it is the rule used to build a candidate, and
it is not the objective of the exact program of \cref{sec:milp}, whose cost is
the person-delay sum of \eqref{eq:Z1} over the whole horizon.  Second, the
proxy is evaluated at the measured arrival rate $q_{i,p}$ and a fixed cycle, so
it cannot price the corridor effects---offsets, spillback, the arrival
response to the split itself---that the program's queue recursion carries.
That is why the sequence choice is made by the controller's own one-cycle
predictor on the program's currency, and why \eqref{eq:rot_green} never appears
as a term of $Z$.

\subsection{Relation to the exact program}
\label{sec:milp_online_map}

The exact program decides the signal plan; the online methods of this appendix decide \emph{action letters} that perturb a launch
schedule one event at a time.  The two are connected by the phase perturbation
each letter induces, which is the same mechanism the program prices through
the queue recursion:
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Letter & Phase perturbation (relative to launch plan $\bar{x}$) & Cycle effect \\
\hline
\texttt{GE} & extends the bus-phase green by $\delta$ steps & $+\delta$ \\
\texttt{INS} & inserts a $\delta$-step bus phase & $+\delta$ \\
\texttt{ER} & truncates the bus-phase green by $\delta$ steps & $-\delta$ \\
\texttt{GR} & moves $\delta$ steps from a donor phase to the bus phase & $0$ \\
\texttt{VP} & skips intermediate phases (zero green on them) & $-\lvert\text{skipped}\rvert$ \\
\texttt{PT} & reorders the phase sequence & $0$ \\
\texttt{OC} & shifts the whole plan by $\Delta$ steps & $0$ net \\
\texttt{NA} & the launch plan itself & $0$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Because the program's decision is the signal plan, none of these letters is a
variable of \cref{sec:milp}; the controller chooses a letter, applies
its phase perturbation to the launch plan, and commits the resulting schedule
for the integer duration it selects.  This is the sense in which the action
space of \cref{sec:actions} is the \emph{candidate generator} for the
program's decision, and the program is the yardstick it is measured against.

