\subsection{The paper method ladder: one adaptive base, four priority layers}
\label{sec:sol_ladder}
% ----------------------------------------------------

The paper reports six methods (Table~\ref{tab:methods}). They are not six
unrelated algorithms: they are one adaptive signal base---the DSW plan
controller---with zero or one bus-priority layer added on top. The comparison is
therefore \emph{within-method}: every difference between two rungs is
attributable to the layer named in the rung, because the plan, the state feed,
the guard set, and the scoring seeds are identical.

\paragraph{The shared base (M1).}
At every decision epoch the controller assembles the estimated state
$\hat{X}_t$ of Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards}: section-total flows, measured
queues, the Kalman bus ETA, the live phase and remaining time, and the POG
Every $\Delta_c=120$\,s it re-selects the coordinated plan from a library
of eight candidate plans
horizon, minimum green 6\,s, main-approach share kept at $0.90$). Candidates
are ranked by a PBIL pressure rollout: the rollout projects the pressure each
candidate would leave on the corridor over the horizon, and the arrival term is
blended at $0.50$ with slope $0.30$. The
DS-dominant weights are loaded once and frozen (learning rate and regulariser
are zero), so the plan cannot drift during a run. The plan's own cycle and
offsets are preserved; no master cycle, split, or offset solver is enabled.
This base contains no bus logic at all: it does not know whether a bus is
present, which is why it commits zero TSP actions.

\paragraph{M0 -- fixed-time reference.}
\texttt{NO\_TSP} runs the commissioned fixed-time plan with the same demand and
seeds, no adaptation and no priority. It is the denominator of every
percentage in the results and the disagreement point of the M4 bargain.

\paragraph{M2 -- learned bus priority (\texttt{MARL\_BUS}).}
The first priority rung adds a CPD-QL head to the base. The head observes a
54-bin state (headway deviation, bus ETA, estimated delay, phase match) and
scores GE/INS through
\begin{equation}
  r(a) = w_h\,\rho_{\text{bus}}\bigl(|\sigma_{\text{in}}| - |\sigma_{\text{out}}(a)|\bigr)
       + (1-w_h)\bigl((d_b^{\text{NA}} - d_b^{a})\,n_b - \Delta_{\text{car}}(a)\bigr),
  \label{eq:cpd_reward}
\end{equation}
with $w_h=0.5$; training uses
$\alpha=0.1$, $\gamma=0.9$ and $\varepsilon=0$ at scoring. The commit is the
Q-argmax, protected only by the analytic cost veto ($\theta=1.5$) and the
shared $Z_4$ cap. Because the reward already prices the cross movement, the
layer is self-limiting: it commits a small number of actions per run and never
rewrites the plan. Its role in the ladder is to show what a learned,
bus-weighted policy does when it is allowed to act on its own value.

\paragraph{M3 -- admission-controlled priority (\texttt{CELLQ\_GATED}).}
The second rung keeps a learned proposal but changes the admission rule. The
head is CellQLearn: a 128-bin CTM state (bus queue, pressure ratio, phase, ETA)
scored by
\begin{equation}
  R(a) = \alpha Z_1^{\text{CTM}}(a) + \beta Z_2^{\text{CTM}}(a)
       + \gamma Z_3^{\text{CTM}}(a),
  \label{eq:cellq_reward}
\end{equation}
where $Z_2^{\text{CTM}}$ is the single-cell cross delay and $Z_3^{\text{CTM}}$
the residual lateness proxy. The commit is \emph{not} the Q-argmax. An
action $a$ is admitted only if its total-passenger bus benefit exceeds the
measured cross cost plus the projected-saturation cascade,
\begin{equation}
  \text{bps}(a) > \text{cpc}^{\text{meas}}(a) + C^{\text{cas}}_a,
  \label{eq:ladder_gate}
\end{equation}
and only inside the recoverable slack $\hat{R}_{i,t}$ of the plan. The minimum
gain is 15\,s and the cost veto is 1.5. This is the only rung whose admission
test is a hard inequality; it turns the unguarded learner from a seed lottery into
a controller that holds the plan when the downstream cost is high.
The net-benefit test and the cascade are detailed next.

\subsubsection{Net-benefit test and projected-saturation cascade cost}
\label{sec:gate_cascade}

The single-cell CTM reward prices the delay an action imposes on the
\emph{immediate} cross approach, but corridor gridlock typically forms two to
three junctions \emph{downstream}, where the released platoon meets a link that
cannot absorb it. Two mechanisms make that network cost visible.

\emph{Measured cross cost.} The cross-street cost of holding a movement on red
is priced from the measured cross-approach state---standing queue plus arrivals
over the extra red---inflated by the superlinear severity
Eq.~\eqref{eq:ctm_severity} as the measured saturation approaches jam; the
larger of the measured and analytic shockwave costs is used, so a congested
cross street is never under-priced.

\emph{Projected-saturation cascade.} The released platoon is propagated
hop-by-hop along the bus route by Eqs.~\eqref{eq:ctm_proj}--\eqref{eq:ctm_carry},
and the cost $C^{\text{cas}}_a$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:ctm_cascade} is charged on the
\emph{projected} saturation after the platoon and the red-time arrivals land,
not on the still-moderate current queue, so gridlock that forms \emph{from} the
release is priced. The guarded commit then applies the balance rule
\cref{eq:rule_balance} in its estimated-state form, admitting $a$ only when the
total-passenger bus benefit $\text{bps}(a)$ (no transit-priority multiplier)
exceeds the measured-plus-cascade cross cost $\text{cpc}(a)$; otherwise the
controller holds.

\paragraph{M4 -- game-theoretic priority (\texttt{GAME\_BUS}).}
The third rung adds a two-player bargain between the bus movement and the cross
movement, with the base plan (\texttt{NO\_ACTION}) as the disagreement point.
The proposer is Harmony Search over integer durations; the commit maximises the
asymmetric Nash product
\begin{equation}
  \max_{a}\; \bigl(\Delta_{\text{bus}}(a)\bigr)^{p_B}
  \bigl(\Delta_{\text{car}}(a)+\tau\bigr)^{p_C},
  \label{eq:ladder_nash}
\end{equation}
with $p_B=p_C=1.0$, subject to the net test and the $Z_4$ cap. The purpose of
this rung is not to outperform M1; it tests whether a non-cooperative bus-vs-cross
bargain can be layered on the plan without breaking it. In the runs it is the
heaviest actor ($\sim$9 commits/run) and the only rung that underperforms
fixed-time ($+7.9\%$ total delay), because the bus-first bargain over-commits where
the released platoon loads a congested cross street.

\paragraph{M5 -- cooperative game priority (\texttt{GAME\_COOP}).}
The fifth rung keeps the Harmony-Search proposer but replaces the two-player
bus-vs-cross bargain with a cooperative (team) payoff over the corridor, so a grant
is admitted only when it improves the joint objective rather than winning a local
bargain. It commits $\sim$1 action/run, defers to the adaptive base, and is the
only priority layer to match the base on total delay ($-4.0\%$) -- the cooperative
framing is what converts the game layer from net-harmful (M4) to net-neutral/base.

\paragraph{Differences between the ladder methods.}
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{0.9cm}p{3.0cm}p{3.9cm}p{4.5cm}p{1.6cm}}
\hline
Rung & Priority layer & Proposal & Admission / commit & Acts/run \\
\hline
M0 & none & fixed-time plan & -- & 0 \\
M1 & none & plan library (PBIL pressure) & apply plan, no decider & 0 \\
M2 & CPD-QL & 54-state Q over GE/INS & Q-argmax + cost veto + $Z_4$ & $\sim$0 \\
M3 & CellQLearn & 128-state Q over GE/INS/GR & net test + cascade + recoverable slack & $\sim$1 \\
M4 & Nash/GAME & HS over integer durations & max Nash product + net test + $Z_4$ & $\sim$9 \\
M5 & Coop Nash/GAME & HS over integer durations (team payoff) & max cooperative product + net test + $Z_4$ & $\sim$1 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\paragraph{Lateness is not a decision variable.}
No rung phases a decision on bus lateness. A bus running behind schedule earns
only the bounded reward bonus of the WOBJ term (a multiplier of at most three
on its reward weight); it does not unlock an action, skip the warm-up, or jump
the admission queue. This is deliberate: the objective is passenger throughput,
and conditioning decisions on lateness silently made earlier priority arms inert for every
on-time bus.

\paragraph{Methods excluded from the evaluation.}
The broader project also implements permissive rule-based priority, ungated
CellQLearn, CPD-QL without bus weighting, LWR/Harmony-Search priority, CTM grid
search, weighted and product Nash bargains without the plan base, passenger
max-pressure, and a per-second centralised controller. They are excluded from
the paper because each either fails to beat the base or degrades it: permissive
rule priority rewrites the plan ($+13.3\%$ on Kelvin Grove), ungated
CellQLearn is a seed lottery ($+4.7\%$), CTM grid search and the standalone Nash
variants gridlock ($+49\%$ and $+55\%$), and max-pressure trades bus delay for
car delay ($+6$--$8\%$). Their derivations, algorithms, and parameter tables
are preserved in the project's excluded-methods note; they are not part of the
paper's claim.

